window.SEARCH_INDEX = {"lessons":[{"slug":"how-the-web-works","num":"1.1","title":"How the Web Works","desc":"Browsers, servers and URLs, what happens when a page loads, and setting up your editor.","props":["URL","404","file://"],"intro":"A website is a folder of files on someone else's computer. Your browser asks for those files one at a time and puts the page together. Knowing that explains most \"it worked on my laptop\" problems.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-read-a-url","title":"Read a URL","text":"Every address has the same parts. Click each part to see what it does. Take apart any URL This uses the same URL parser your browser uses. Part 2"},{"id":"sec-one-page-many-requests","title":"One page, many requests","text":"Load the page and watch the log. The browser gets the HTML first, reads it, and only then discovers the CSS, images and fonts it also needs. Break something to see what happens. Load the page Break something The HTML links Style.css, but the file is style.css The photo is a 5 MB file straight off a phone The home page is named Home.html, not index.html Request times are made up, but the order is real. Open DevTools and its Network tab on any site to see the real list. Network log trailclub.github.io/ Part 3"},{"id":"sec-set-up-your-computer","title":"Set up your computer","text":"You don't need a server to start. A text editor and a browser are enough. 1 Install a code editor VS Code is free and works on every computer. It colors your code and closes tags for you. Don't use Word or TextEdit's rich text mode: they add hidden formatting. 2 Make one project folder Put every file for the site inside it, with index.html at the top level. Open the whole folder in your editor (File → Open Folder), not single files. 3 Open the page in a browser Double-click index.html, or drag it into a browser window. Save in the editor, then refresh the browser to see each change. 4 Optional: a local server The VS Code extension Live Server serves your folder at http://127.0.0.1:5500 and refreshes the page every time you save. It behaves more like the real web than double-clicking. Address bar starts with Where the files come from Watch out for file:///Users/you/site/ Straight from your own disk. No server involved. Paths starting with / point to the root of your hard drive, so they break. http://127.0.0.1:5500/ A small server running on your own computer (127.0.0.1 always means \"this computer\"). Only you can see it. It stops when you close the editor. https://you.github.io/site/ A real web server anyone can reach. File names are case-sensitive here, even if they weren't on your laptop."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"The browser is the client. The computer holding the files is the server. The browser sends a request, and the server sends back a response with a status code. 200 means OK. 404 means the server has no file at that path. 301 means it moved. Each file is its own request. The HTML arrives first, and the browser finds the rest by reading it. A domain name like github.io is a human-friendly label. DNS looks up the server's numeric address behind it. When a URL ends in a folder (/hikes/), the server sends that folder's index.html. https encrypts the connection. Modern browsers warn visitors about plain http sites."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Editing a file, forgetting to save, and wondering why the browser hasn't changed. Opening a single file in the editor instead of the project folder, then saving new files in the wrong place. Testing only with file://, then finding broken images online because of capital letters in file names. Thinking the page is one file. If the CSS or an image fails to load, the HTML still shows up without it."}]},{"slug":"html-syntax","num":"1.2","title":"HTML Syntax","desc":"Elements, tags and attributes, plus how whitespace and special characters behave.","props":["<tag>","attribute","&amp;"],"intro":"HTML is made of elements, and elements are made of tags, attributes and content. Learn the names for each part, then see how the browser treats spaces and special characters.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-anatomy-of-an-element","title":"Anatomy of an element","text":"Click any part, or pick a name below. Empty (void) elements have no closing tag <img><br><hr><input><meta><link> They can't hold content, so there's nothing to close. Writing <img /> with a slash is allowed but not needed. Part 2"},{"id":"sec-whitespace-collapses","title":"Whitespace collapses","text":"Type extra spaces, tabs and blank lines. The browser squashes every run of whitespace into a single space. What you type in your HTML What the browser shows To start a new paragraph, use a new <p>. Use CSS for spacing, not lots of spaces or <br>s. Part 3"},{"id":"sec-special-characters","title":"Special characters","text":"Some characters mean something to HTML, like < which starts a tag. To show them as text, write an entity. Type below to see what needs escaping. Text you want to show Write this in your HTML Common entities Shows Write Why < &lt; Would start a tag > &gt; Pairs with &lt; & &amp; Would start an entity \" &quot; Inside attribute values © &copy; Easier to type &nbsp; A space that never breaks the line"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Elements nest like boxes inside boxes. Close the inner one before the outer one. Attributes go in the opening tag, as name=\"value\". Always quote the value. Tag and attribute names aren't case-sensitive, but write them in lowercase. Comments look like <!-- like this --> and don't appear on the page."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Forgetting a closing tag, so everything after it gets the same formatting. Curly \"smart quotes\" pasted from a word processor around attribute values. Spaces around the equals sign in odd places, or missing quotes around values with spaces. Using lots of &nbsp; or <br> to push things around. That's CSS's job."}]},{"slug":"document-structure","num":"1.3","title":"Document Structure","desc":"The skeleton every page needs: doctype, head, body, and how they nest.","props":["<!DOCTYPE>","<head>","<meta>"],"intro":"Every HTML page starts with the same skeleton. Click any line to learn what it does, then remove it to see what breaks.","more":"On a phone How it nests <html> <head> info about the page, not shown meta · title · link <body> everything people see h1 · p · img · nav · …","sections":[{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"<head> holds information about the page. <body> holds what visitors see. Every element you open, you close, and tags close in the reverse order they opened. Indent nested elements so you can see the structure at a glance. Save the file as index.html for the main page of a site. Servers look for that name first."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Putting visible content like <h1> inside <head>. Having two <body> elements, or content after </html>. Leaving out the viewport meta tag, so phones show a tiny zoomed-out page. Linking a stylesheet with the wrong path, like href=\"style.css\" when the file is in a css/ folder."}]},{"slug":"head-metadata","num":"1.4","title":"The Head & Metadata","desc":"Page titles, favicons, meta descriptions, and the preview card when a link is shared.","props":["<title>","favicon","og:image"],"intro":"Nothing in the <head> shows on the page, but it decides how your site looks everywhere else: the browser tab, search results, and the preview card when someone shares a link.","more":"<head> <title> meta description link rel=\"icon\" Open Graph tags Browser tab New Tab Search result Shared in a chat or social post Copy Favicons","sections":[{"id":"sec-make-the-little-tab-icon","title":"Make the little tab icon","text":"A favicon is a small square image. One SVG covers most browsers. Add a PNG for phones that save your site to the home screen. 1. Make a square image Use a simple shape or one or two letters. Fine detail disappears at 16 × 16 pixels. Save it as favicon.svg, or as a 32 × 32 favicon.png. 2. Link it in the head <link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.svg\" type=\"image/svg+xml\"> 3. Add a home-screen icon <link rel=\"apple-touch-icon\" href=\"icon-180.png\"> A 180 × 180 PNG, used when someone adds your site to their phone's home screen."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Give every page its own <title>, most specific part first: \"Royal Arch Trail | Boulder Trail Club\". It's also what bookmarks and screen readers announce. The meta description is a one-sentence pitch of about 150 characters. Search engines may show it under your title. Open Graph (og:) tags control the preview card in chats and social posts. Without them, apps guess, or show a bare link. og:image needs a full https:// URL, not a relative path, because the image is fetched by another company's server. A good share image is 1200 × 630 pixels."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Copying one page to start another and leaving the same title on every page. Putting <meta> or <link> tags in the body. They only work in the head. Expecting <meta name=\"keywords\"> to help. Search engines have ignored it for years. Changing the favicon and not seeing it. Browsers cache favicons hard: try a private window."}]},{"slug":"text-semantics","num":"1.5","title":"Text Semantics","desc":"Headings, strong vs b, em vs i, quotes, code and more, chosen for meaning.","props":["<h1>–<h6>","<strong>","<blockquote>"],"intro":"Elements like <strong> and <em> say what text means, and the look comes from that meaning. Click any highlighted text in this trail report to see which element it uses and why.","more":"Show the tags Break the headings Heading outline (what screen reader users skim) Practice","sections":[{"id":"sec-which-element","title":"Which element?","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"One <h1> per page, then <h2> for sections and <h3> inside those. Don't skip levels. <strong> means important and <em> means stressed. <b> and <i> only change the look. Pick elements for meaning, then change the look with CSS. A heading can be small; strong text doesn't have to be bold. Use <p> for each paragraph instead of <br><br> between them."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Picking <h4> because you want smaller text. Making a fake heading with bold text in a <p>. Using <blockquote> just to indent something. Wrapping whole paragraphs in <strong>. When everything is important, nothing is."}]},{"slug":"lists","num":"1.6","title":"Lists","desc":"Unordered, ordered and description lists, nesting, and styling markers.","props":["<ul>","<ol>","<dl>"],"intro":"HTML has three kinds of list. Choose the one that matches your content, then use CSS to change how the markers look, or remove them to build a menu.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-pick-the-right-list","title":"Pick the right list","text":"<ul> <ol> <dl> <ol> attributes start type 1, 2, 3 a, b, c A, B, C i, ii, iii I, II, III reversed Include a nested list Part 2"},{"id":"sec-style-the-markers","title":"Style the markers","text":"The same unordered list, restyled with CSS. The last option turns it into a navigation menu, which is how most site menus are built. list-style-type disc circle square decimal lower-alpha upper-roman \"→ \" (any text) none list-style-position outside inside padding-left Color the markers with ::marker Turn it into a menu Trails Maps Conditions Gear checklist"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"<ul> when order doesn't matter, <ol> when it does, <dl> for terms and their descriptions. A list's only direct children are <li>s (or <dt>/<dd> in a dl). To nest a list, put the inner list inside an <li>, after its text. Navigation menus are usually a <ul> inside a <nav>, with bullets removed."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Putting a nested <ul> directly inside the outer <ul> instead of inside an <li>. Typing your own numbers or dashes into list items. Removing bullets but not the default padding-left, leaving a mysterious indent. Using <br> between lines instead of real list items."}]},{"slug":"semantic-elements","num":"1.7","title":"Semantic Elements","desc":"When to use header, nav, main, section, and article instead of div.","props":["<main>","<nav>","<section>"],"intro":"Semantic elements describe what content is, not how it looks. They look the same as a plain div, but screen readers, search engines and other developers can tell a navigation menu from a footer.","more":"Semantic HTML \"Div soup\" Click a region to see what it means. Practice","sections":[{"id":"sec-which-element-fits","title":"Which element fits?","text":"Pick the best element for each piece of content."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Use <div> only when no other element describes the content. It means \"a box, nothing more.\" One <main> per page, holding the content that's unique to that page. <article> makes sense on its own (a blog post, a product card). <section> is a themed part of something bigger and usually has a heading. Semantic elements don't change how things look. You still style them with CSS."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Choosing an element for its default look, like <h3> because the text is small. Style the size with CSS instead. Making a clickable <div> instead of a <button>. Keyboards and screen readers can't use it. Wrapping every group of links in <nav>. Save it for major navigation. Skipping heading levels (h1 to h4) to get a smaller font."}]},{"slug":"links-paths","num":"1.8","title":"Links & Paths","desc":"Relative paths, ../, and why a link works on your laptop but not online.","props":["href","../","#anchor"],"intro":"A relative path is directions from the file you're writing in to the file you want. Pick where you are, click where you want to go, then try writing the path yourself.","more":"I'm writing code in index.html projects/trail-app.html projects/notes/week1.html css/style.css Click a file to link to it Type the path you'd write Show the answer How to read it Reference","sections":[{"id":"sec-kinds-of-links","title":"Kinds of links","text":"Relative path images/logo.png../about.html Starts from the current file's folder. Use this for files in your own site. Absolute URL https://developer.mozilla.org The full address. Use it for other websites. Root-relative /images/logo.png Starts at the top of the domain. Breaks on GitHub Pages project sites and when opening files directly, so avoid it in this class. Same-page anchor #contact Jumps to the element with id=\"contact\" on this page. Email mailto:you@example.com Opens the visitor's email app, if they have one set up."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"../ means \"go up one folder.\" Use it once per level you need to climb. A path in a CSS file, like url(...), starts from the CSS file's folder, not the HTML page's. Link text should say where the link goes. \"Read the trail guide\" beats \"click here.\" Opening a link in a new tab with target=\"_blank\" is fine for outside sites, but don't do it for every link."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Wrong capitalization: Trail-Photo.jpg isn't Trail-Photo.JPG. Your laptop may forgive it, but web servers usually won't. Spaces in file names, which turn into %20 and break easily. Use dashes. Paths from your own computer, like C:\\Users\\... or file:///. They only work on your machine. Backslashes. Web paths always use forward slashes /."}]},{"slug":"tables","num":"1.9","title":"Tables","desc":"Header cells, spanning cells and footers for real data, and when not to use a table.","props":["<th>","colspan","scope"],"intro":"Tables are for data with rows and columns, like a schedule or a comparison. Build up this table one feature at a time, and click any cell to hear what a screen reader says.","more":"HTML Header cells use <th scope> Add a <caption> CSS border-collapse (merge double borders) padding in cells Zebra stripes with :nth-child(even) Right-align numbers Turn everything on Click a cell in the table. Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-cells-that-span-footers-and-column-groups","title":"Cells that span, footers and column groups","text":"Some cells need to stretch across columns or down rows. Turn each feature on to see the problem it solves. rowspan=\"2\" on the long hike colspan=\"2\" on the closure Add a <tfoot> <colgroup> to highlight Sunday Practice"},{"id":"sec-table-or-not","title":"Table or not?","text":"Tables used to be used for page layout. Now they're only for data. Decide for each one."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Structure: <table> holds rows (<tr>), and rows hold cells (<td> for data, <th> for headers). Group rows with <thead> and <tbody>. scope=\"col\" or scope=\"row\" tells screen readers which cells a header labels. colspan and rowspan merge cells. Every row must still add up to the same number of columns. Wrap wide tables in a container with overflow-x: auto so they scroll on phones instead of breaking the page."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Using a table to lay out a page or a grid of cards. Use Flexbox or Grid. Making header cells bold <td>s instead of real <th>s. A different number of cells in each row, which shifts columns out of line. Putting a border on the table only and expecting the cells to get one too."}]},{"slug":"forms","num":"1.10","title":"Forms & Inputs","desc":"Labels, input types, fieldsets, validation rules, and where the data goes.","props":["<label>","<fieldset>","method"],"intro":"Picking the right input type gets you validation, phone keyboards and date pickers for free. Connecting each label to its input makes the form usable for everyone.","more":"Pick an input type What you get for free Labels","sections":[{"id":"sec-click-each-label","title":"Click each label","text":"A label connected with for and id moves focus to its input when clicked. Screen readers also read it aloud when the input is focused. Connected Your name Email me updates Click the label text, not the box. Not connected Your name Email me updates Click the label text, not the box. Validation"},{"id":"sec-let-the-browser-check-it","title":"Let the browser check it","text":"Attributes like required, type=\"email\" and min make the browser block bad submissions before your code runs. Try submitting this with empty or wrong values. Name * Email * Age (13 to 120) Sign up Grouping"},{"id":"sec-fieldset-and-legend","title":"fieldset and legend","text":"A group of radio buttons or checkboxes needs a question. Wrap the group in a <fieldset> and put the question in its <legend>. Focus a radio button in each version to hear the difference. With fieldset and legend Trail difficulty Easy Moderate Hard Focus a radio button. Without Trail difficulty Easy Moderate Hard Focus a radio button. More controls"},{"id":"sec-datalist-progress-and-meter","title":"datalist, progress and meter","text":"<datalist> Trail name Suggestions appear as you type, but people can still enter anything. <progress> Upload progress 40% Shows how far a task has got. Leave out value for an \"in progress, unknown amount\" bar. <meter> Trail difficulty: 5 / 10 A measurement within a known range. low, high and optimum make the browser color it good, okay or bad. Validation attributes"},{"id":"sec-set-the-rules-let-the-browser-explain-them","title":"Set the rules, let the browser explain them","text":"Type in each field. The messages below come straight from your browser. Username required minlength=\"3\" maxlength=\"15\" ZIP code pattern=\"[0-9]{5}\" Group size type=\"number\" min=\"1\" max=\"12\" Sending data"},{"id":"sec-where-does-the-data-go","title":"Where does the data go?","text":"A form's action says where to send the data and method says how. Each field is sent as name=value, so a field with no name is left out. Fill this in and watch the request it would make. Nothing is actually sent from this page. method=\"get\" method=\"post\" Trail name=\"trail\" Mesa Trail Royal Arch Date name=\"date\" Group size name=\"size\" Notes (no name attribute) The request"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Every input needs a label. The label's for must match the input's id exactly. The name attribute is what the data is called when the form is sent. Radio buttons in the same group share one name, so only one can be picked. Wrap the group in a <fieldset> with a <legend>. Use <button type=\"submit\"> to send a form and type=\"button\" for anything else."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Using placeholder instead of a label. It disappears when you type and is often low-contrast. Typing type=\"text\" for everything and missing the free validation and keyboards. Reusing the same id twice, so a label points at the wrong input. Forgetting that browser validation doesn't protect a server. Real apps check on the server too."}]},{"slug":"images","num":"1.11","title":"Images & Alt Text","desc":"Adding images, writing useful alt text, and captioning with figure and figcaption.","props":["<img>","alt","<figure>"],"intro":"The alt attribute is what people hear when they can't see an image. Pick alt text for each image in this article, then switch to the screen reader view to hear the result.","more":"Choose alt text What you see What a screen reader says Image failed to load Captions","sections":[{"id":"sec-figure-and-figcaption","title":"figure and figcaption","text":"When an image needs a visible caption, wrap the image and its caption in a <figure> and put the caption in <figcaption>. That ties them together for screen readers and for CSS. img + p figure + figcaption The markup What a screen reader says alt text vs caption alt replaces the image for people who can't see it. A caption is visible to everyone and adds information, like who took the photo or when. Don't repeat the same words in both: a screen reader would read them twice."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Describe what the image means in context, not every detail. Ask: what would I say if I read this page aloud? Purely decorative images get an empty alt=\"\" so screen readers skip them. Don't leave alt out entirely. For an image inside a link, describe where the link goes (\"Trailhead home\"), not the picture. For charts, give the main takeaway in the alt and put the full data in nearby text or a table. Use <figure> and <figcaption> when an image has a visible caption. Always set width and height attributes so the page doesn't jump around while images load."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Starting with \"Image of…\". Screen readers already announce that it's an image. Using the file name, like IMG_4031.jpg, as the alt text. Missing alt completely. Many screen readers then read out the file name. Wrong paths: src=\"images/photo.JPG\" won't load photo.jpg on most servers, because file names are case-sensitive."}]},{"slug":"image-formats","num":"1.12","title":"Image Formats","desc":"JPG vs PNG vs SVG vs WebP, real file sizes, and resizing before you upload.","props":["JPG","SVG","WebP"],"intro":"The same picture can be a 30 KB file or a 900 KB file depending on its format. Photos, logos and screenshots each have a format that suits them best.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-same-image-different-files","title":"Same image, different files","text":"Your browser is really encoding these images right now, so the file sizes are real. Pick a kind of image, then compare the formats. The zoom shows the damage compression does. kind of image photo logo screenshot quality (JPG and WebP) PNG is lossless: it keeps every pixel exactly. JPG and WebP are lossy: they throw away detail you probably won't notice, and lower quality throws away more. file size · 400 × 250 px Full image Zoomed in 4× Cheat sheet"},{"id":"sec-which-format-when","title":"Which format, when","text":"JPG .jpg · lossy Use for photos. Avoid for logos, text and anything needing a transparent background. JPG has no transparency. PNG .png · lossless Use for screenshots, flat graphics with text, and images with transparent backgrounds. Avoid for photos. They come out 5–10× bigger than a JPG. WebP .webp · either Use for almost anything. Usually 25–35% smaller than JPG or PNG at the same quality, and supports transparency. Note Every current browser supports it. Some older desktop apps can't open it. AVIF .avif · either Use for photos when you want the smallest file. Often half the size of a JPG. Note Slower to export, and not every editor can save it yet. Offer a JPG fallback with <picture>. SVG .svg · vector Use for logos, icons and simple illustrations. It's math, not pixels, so it stays sharp at any size and is often tiny. Avoid for photos. They can't be drawn as shapes. GIF .gif · old Use for almost nothing now. Only 256 colors. Instead for an animation, use a short muted MP4 in a <video>. It's often 10× smaller. Part 2"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-format","title":"Pick the format","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Resize before you upload. Export about twice the width the image appears on screen, so it stays sharp on high-density screens. A photo shown 600px wide needs about 1200px, not the 4000px from your phone. Then compress it. Free tools like Squoosh (squoosh.app) and ImageOptim let you compare quality settings side by side. Quality 70–80 is usually indistinguishable from 100. A good target for a large photo is under 200 KB. Most icons and logos should be a few KB. An SVG can go in an <img> like any other image, or be pasted into the HTML directly so CSS can recolor it."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Saving a logo as JPG, which leaves blurry smudges around the letters and a white box instead of transparency. Saving a photo as PNG \"for quality,\" and getting a file several times bigger with no visible difference. Renaming photo.png to photo.jpg. That changes the name, not the format. Re-export it instead. Re-saving the same JPG over and over. Each save loses a little more detail. Keep an original and export copies."}]},{"slug":"video-audio","num":"1.13","title":"Video & Audio","desc":"Player attributes, why autoplay gets blocked, and adding captions.","props":["<video>","controls","<track>"],"intro":"The <video> and <audio> elements play media without any plugins. Their attributes control what visitors see and whether anything plays by itself. This player is a simulation that follows the same rules as a real one.","more":"<video> attributes controls poster=\"cover.jpg\" autoplay muted loop Child elements <track kind=\"captions\"> Reload the page Captions","sections":[{"id":"sec-the-captions-file","title":"The captions file","text":"Captions live in a separate WebVTT text file, linked with <track>. Each cue has a start time, an end time and the text. The current cue is highlighted while the video plays."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Always include controls unless you build your own play button. Without it, people can't pause or adjust volume. Browsers block autoplay with sound. Autoplay only works with muted, and should be rare. List more than one <source> (like MP4 and WebM) and the browser plays the first one it supports. Add captions for videos and a transcript for audio, for people who can't hear it and everyone watching on mute. <audio controls> works the same way, without the picture."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Uploading a huge video file straight from a phone. Compress it, or host it on YouTube or Vimeo and embed it. Expecting autoplay to work without muted. No poster, so the player shows a black box until it loads. Background videos that loop forever with no way to pause them."}]},{"slug":"embeds","num":"1.14","title":"Embeds & iframes","desc":"Put YouTube videos and Google Maps on your page, and make them fit on phones.","props":["<iframe>","title","aspect-ratio"],"intro":"An <iframe> is a window in your page that shows another web page. It's how you put a YouTube video, a Google Map or a CodePen demo on your site without hosting it yourself.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-build-an-embed","title":"Build an embed","text":"Sites hand you embed code with a fixed width and height. Make the page narrow like a phone, then fix the embed so it fits and is accessible. embed YouTube video Google Map CodePen demo page width fix it title=\"…\" responsive CSS loading=\"lazy\" The embeds here are pictures of the real thing, so this page works offline. Hike of the week: Royal Arch Copy Part 2"},{"id":"sec-a-page-inside-a-page","title":"A page inside a page","text":"An iframe holds a completely separate document. Your CSS doesn't reach inside it, and its CSS doesn't leak out. Style the outer page and watch. your page Trail Club This paragraph is on your page. Embedded page This paragraph lives in a different document, inside the iframe. \"> Apply p, h3 { color: red; text-transform: uppercase } Click the button to add a rule to your page's stylesheet. This is why you can't restyle a YouTube player with your own CSS. You can only size and position the iframe box itself. Part 3"},{"id":"sec-where-to-get-the-code","title":"Where to get the code","text":"YouTube Open the video and click Share. Choose Embed and copy the code. Paste it into your HTML, then add a title and the responsive CSS. Google Maps Search for the place and click Share. Pick the Embed a map tab and copy the HTML. It already has loading=\"lazy\". Change the title and remove the fixed width. When embedding is refused Many sites, including most banks, social sites and Google Search, tell browsers not to show them inside iframes. You'll see a blank box or \"refused to connect.\" Link to them instead."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Give every iframe a title that says what's in it. Screen readers announce it so people can decide whether to go in. To make an embed responsive, set width: 100%, height: auto and an aspect-ratio that matches the content, like 16 / 9 for video. loading=\"lazy\" waits until the visitor scrolls near the embed. A YouTube player downloads close to 1 MB before anyone presses play. Each embed is a full web page with its own scripts and tracking. Use them where they add real value."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Pasting the code with width=\"560\" and never testing on a phone, so the video sticks out of the page. Pasting the page address (youtube.com/watch?v=…) as the src. It must be the embed address (youtube.com/embed/…). Trying to style what's inside the iframe with your own CSS. Embedding a video you could simply host yourself with <video>, or vice versa: uploading a huge video file to GitHub Pages instead of YouTube."}]},{"slug":"css-basics","num":"2.1","title":"How CSS Is Applied","desc":"The parts of a rule, and external vs internal vs inline styles.","props":["selector","declaration","<link>"],"intro":"A CSS rule picks some elements and gives them styles. Learn the names for each part of a rule, then see the three places CSS can live and which one wins when they disagree.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-anatomy-of-a-rule","title":"Anatomy of a rule","text":"Click any part of the rule, or pick a name below. Part 2"},{"id":"sec-three-places-to-put-css","title":"Three places to put CSS","text":"All three below try to color the same heading. Turn them on and off and swap the order of the two in the head. In the <head>, the Swap order Trail Report"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Use an external stylesheet for real projects. One file can style every page of the site. Each declaration ends with a semicolon. The last one technically doesn't need it, but always add it. Comments look like /* like this */. Use them to label sections of your stylesheet. CSS ignores extra spaces and line breaks, so format rules one declaration per line for readability."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"A <link> with the wrong path, so no styles load at all. Check the Network tab in DevTools. Writing CSS inside the <body> without a <style> tag, so it shows up as text on the page. Using inline style=\"\" everywhere, which is hard to change and beats your stylesheet. HTML-style comments (<!-- -->) inside a CSS file."}]},{"slug":"selectors","num":"2.2","title":"Selectors & Specificity","desc":"Classes, IDs, combinators and attribute selectors, and why one rule beats another.","props":[".class","h2 + p","[href]"],"intro":"A selector picks which elements a rule applies to. Type one below and the matching elements light up. When two rules disagree, specificity decides which one wins.","more":"Selector Try these Mesa Trail 6.7 miles Moderate Water Bear spray Map Details PDF map Royal Arch 3.4 miles Hard Details Trail data updated Tuesday Reference","sections":[{"id":"sec-combinators-and-attribute-selectors","title":"Combinators and attribute selectors","text":"Each of these is in the \"Try these\" list above. Combinators: how elements relate .card p space Descendant: a p anywhere inside .card .card > a > Child: an a directly inside .card h2 + p + Adjacent sibling: the p right after an h2 h2 ~ a ~ General sibling: any a after an h2, same parent Attribute selectors [href] has the attribute [type=\"email\"] exactly equals [href^=\"https\"] starts with [href$=\".pdf\"] ends with [class*=\"card\"] contains anywhere Specificity"},{"id":"sec-which-rule-wins","title":"Which rule wins?","text":"Both rules below try to color the same heading. The browser compares their scores column by column, from left to right. If the scores tie, the rule written later wins. Rule A (written first) Rule B (written second) Mesa Trail"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"A space means \"inside, at any depth.\" > means \"a direct child only.\" No space between parts means \"both at once\": .tag.hard is an element with both classes. A comma makes a list: h2, .btn matches either one. + and ~ only look forward at later siblings, never back up or into children. Score order: IDs beat classes, classes beat elements. One ID outranks any number of classes. Inline style=\"\" beats all selectors, and !important beats that. Avoid both when you can."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Forgetting the dot: card looks for a <card> element, not class=\"card\". Writing .card.title when you meant .card .title. One space changes everything. Styling with IDs everywhere, then needing even stronger selectors to override them. Reaching for !important instead of finding out why a rule is losing."}]},{"slug":"pseudo-classes","num":"2.3","title":"Pseudo-classes & States","desc":"Hover, focus and link states, nth-child, and building a nav menu.","props":[":hover",":focus-visible",":nth-child"],"intro":"A pseudo-class, written with one colon, selects an element when it's in a certain state or position. Hover, click, tab and type on the controls below and watch which states switch on.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-interactive-states","title":"Interactive states","text":"button Save trail button disabled Sold out aView the trail map input type=\"email\" required input type=\"checkbox\" Dogs allowed Point at or tab to a control Try clicking the button with the mouse, then reaching it with the Tab key. Only the keyboard shows the :focus-visible ring. Part 2"},{"id":"sec-position-nth-child","title":"Position: :nth-child()","text":"The formula an+b is calculated for n = 0, 1, 2, 3... and every result picks an item. li:nth-child( ) Part 3"},{"id":"sec-styling-links-and-building-a-menu","title":"Styling links and building a menu","text":"Links have four states. Write them in this order, or later ones get overridden: :link, :visited, :hover, :active. A common memory trick is \"LoVe HAte.\" Then add :focus-visible for keyboard users. Build up a menu one step at a time below. Remove bullets and indent Put the links in a row (flex) Make each link a padded block :hover and :focus-visible styles Mark the current page with aria-current Turn everything on Home Trails Maps Gear"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Pseudo-classes use one colon (:hover). Pseudo-elements like ::before use two. Style :hover and :focus-visible together so keyboard users get the same feedback as mouse users. Phones have no hover. Never hide something important behind :hover alone. :nth-child(odd) and (even) are great for striped tables and lists. For security, browsers only let :visited change colors, nothing else."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Removing focus outlines with outline: none and adding nothing back, so keyboard users get lost. A space before the colon: a :hover means \"any hovered element inside an a.\" Expecting p:first-child to pick the first p. It picks a p only if it's the very first child of its parent. Showing :invalid styles before the user has typed anything."}]},{"slug":"pseudo-elements","num":"2.4","title":"Pseudo-elements","desc":"Decorate with ::before and ::after, drop caps, placeholders and selections.","props":["::before","content","::first-letter"],"intro":"Pseudo-elements, written with two colons, style a part of an element, or add decorative content before or after it, without changing your HTML. Switch each one on and off to see what it adds.","more":"","sections":[{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"::before and ::after need a content property, even if it's empty (content: \"\"), or they don't appear. They're inserted inside the element, as its first and last child. Use them for decoration: quote marks, icons, underlines, arrows. Don't put important information in them. Two colons mark a pseudo-element (::before). One colon is a pseudo-class (:hover)."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Forgetting content, then wondering why ::after does nothing. Trying ::before on an <img> or <input>. Elements with no content inside can't have them. Putting real text in content. It can't be selected and some screen readers skip it. Expecting ::first-letter to work on an inline element like a span."}]},{"slug":"cascade","num":"2.5","title":"Cascade & Inheritance","desc":"Which styles pass down to children, and how the browser settles conflicts.","props":["inherit","!important","order"],"intro":"Some styles pass down from parent to child automatically, and some don't. When several rules disagree, the cascade decides which one wins.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-gets-inherited","title":"What gets inherited?","text":"Set a property on the article. The badges read what the browser actually computed for each child. Set on article button, input { font: inherit; color: inherit } article h3 Mesa Trail p Rolling foothills with views of the Flatirons. aTrail map button Save trail input Part 2"},{"id":"sec-which-rule-wins","title":"Which rule wins?","text":"Three rules all try to color the same heading, <h3 class=\"title\" id=\"top\">. Change the selectors, reorder the rules, or add !important. Mesa Trail The browser checks in this order !important beats everything without it. Then higher specificity wins (IDs, then classes, then elements). If it's still a tie, the rule that comes later in the CSS wins."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Text properties inherit: color, font-*, line-height, text-align. Box properties don't: border, padding, margin, background, width. Set your fonts and text color once on body and let everything inherit them. Any property can be forced to inherit with the value inherit."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Wondering why buttons and inputs ignore your font and color. They don't inherit them unless you add font: inherit and color: inherit. Thinking a background was inherited. Children are transparent, so you're seeing the parent through them. Fixing a losing rule with !important instead of checking order and specificity in DevTools. Linking two stylesheets in the wrong order, so the general one overrides the specific one."}]},{"slug":"box-model","num":"2.6","title":"The Box Model","desc":"Content, padding, border, margin, and why your 300px box is 352px wide.","props":["padding","margin","box-sizing"],"intro":"Every element is a box made of four layers: content, padding, border, and margin. Adjust each layer and see how much space the box really takes up.","more":".box box-sizing content-box border-box width padding border-width margin content","sections":[{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"By default (content-box), width sets only the content. Padding and border are added on top. With border-box, width includes padding and border, so the box stays the size you asked for. Margin is space outside the border. It's transparent and never part of the width. Most developers start every stylesheet with * { box-sizing: border-box; }."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Setting width: 100% plus padding and getting a horizontal scrollbar. Using padding when you wanted space between boxes (that's margin or gap). Being surprised when two vertical margins collapse into one instead of adding together. Forgetting the browser's default margin on body, h1, and p."}]},{"slug":"display","num":"2.7","title":"Display Types","desc":"Block vs inline vs inline-block, and why width does nothing on a span.","props":["block","inline","inline-block"],"intro":"Every element is either block (starts on a new line, fills the width) or inline (flows inside text like a word). That's why width works on a div and does nothing on a span.","more":".box (three of them) display inline block inline-block width height margin (top and bottom) padding Pack water and snacks before you set out, and check the forecast because afternoon storms roll in fast above treeline. Hiding things","sections":[{"id":"sec-display-none-vs-visibility-hidden-vs-opacity-0","title":"display: none vs visibility: hidden vs opacity: 0","text":"All three make card 2 invisible. Watch what happens to the space it took up. Visible display: none visibility: hidden opacity: 0 1 2 3 display: noneGone completely. The space closes up and screen readers skip it. visibility: hiddenInvisible, but its space stays. Screen readers skip it. opacity: 0Invisible but still there: it keeps its space, can be clicked, and screen readers still read it."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Block by default: div, p, h1–h6, ul, section. Inline by default: span, a, strong, em, img. inline-block flows like a word but accepts width, height and vertical margins. It's handy for buttons and badges. display: flex and grid change how an element's children are laid out. The element itself acts like a block. Changing display never changes what an element means. A span with display: block is still a span."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Setting width or height on a link or span and wondering why nothing happens. Adding top margin to an inline element to push it down. It's ignored. Putting a div inside a p. The browser closes the paragraph early and breaks your layout. Mysterious small gaps between inline-block items. That's the space character between them in the HTML."}]},{"slug":"sizing","num":"2.8","title":"Sizing Items","desc":"Fixed vs flexible widths, min and max limits, aspect-ratio and vh.","props":["max-width","min-height","aspect-ratio"],"intro":"Without a set size, a block fills the width it's given and is as tall as its content. Set sizes with care: fixed pixels break on small screens, while percentages and min/max limits adapt.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-width-fixed-flexible-and-limited","title":"Width: fixed, flexible and limited","text":"Drag the parent's width and compare how each box responds. The numbers are measured live. parent width Watch the 400px box when the parent gets narrower than 400px. no width (auto) width: 50% width: 400px width: 100%; max-width: 400px width: 30%; min-width: 180px fit-content Part 2"},{"id":"sec-height-vs-min-height","title":"height vs min-height","text":"Add more text and see which box copes. paragraphs of text height: 120px Content below the box min-height: 120px Content below the box Part 3"},{"id":"sec-aspect-ratio-and-viewport-units","title":"aspect-ratio and viewport units","text":"aspect-ratio 16 / 9 4 / 3 1 / 1 3 / 4 width height in vh"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"width: 100%; max-width: 600px; is the go-to pattern: full width on phones, capped on big screens. Percentage widths are a share of the parent's width. Let height come from the content. Reach for min-height if you need a minimum. aspect-ratio keeps a box's shape as its width changes, great for video and image placeholders. vh is a percentage of the browser window's height. 100vh is the full screen."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Big fixed widths like width: 1000px that overflow on phones. A percentage height that does nothing because the parent has no set height. Fixed heights on text boxes, so text spills out. Using 100vh on phones, where browser toolbars make it slightly too tall. 100dvh adjusts for them."}]},{"slug":"overflow","num":"2.9","title":"Overflow","desc":"Content that doesn't fit, and hunting down sideways scrolling.","props":["overflow","text-overflow","overflow-wrap"],"intro":"Overflow happens when content is bigger than its box. CSS doesn't hide it by default; it lets it spill out so you notice. Here's how to control it, and how to track down the element that makes a whole page scroll sideways.","more":"overflow visible hidden scroll auto clip height Use min-height instead of height Title: one line with an ellipsis (…) Long link: overflow-wrap: anywhere Chautauqua to Royal Arch via the Bluebell Mesa Trail Climb through ponderosa pines to a natural sandstone arch with views across Boulder. The last half mile is steep, rocky and has over 200 stone steps. https://example.org/trails/boulder/royal-arch-via-bluebell-mesa-trail-map Bring water: there's none on the trail. Practice","sections":[{"id":"sec-find-what-s-making-the-page-scroll-sideways","title":"Find what's making the page scroll sideways","text":"This phone-sized page scrolls left and right, which is a classic bug. Click each part of the page to inspect its width, find the one wider than the screen, then fix it. Trailhead Fall hours: trailheads open at 6am. Parking fills by 8am on weekends. Trail · Miles · Gain · Difficulty · Dogs · Shade · Water MapSave Inspector Click a part of the page on the left."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"overflow: auto adds a scrollbar only when needed. It's usually the best choice. hidden and clip cut content off. Make sure nothing important is lost. Prefer min-height to height for boxes with text, so they can grow. Long URLs and words don't wrap by themselves. overflow-wrap: anywhere lets them break."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Fixed pixel widths, like width: 380px, wider than a phone screen. Putting overflow: hidden on body to hide the sideways scroll instead of fixing the cause. Fixed height on a text box that later gets more text, translated text, or larger font settings. Using overflow: scroll, which shows scrollbars even when nothing overflows."}]},{"slug":"colors-units","num":"2.10","title":"Colors & Units","desc":"px, rem, %, vw, and the many ways to write a color.","props":["rem","vw","hsl()"],"intro":"CSS gives you several ways to write the same color and many units for size. Mix a color with the sliders, then see how each unit turns into real pixels.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-mixing-colors-with-hsl","title":"Mixing colors with HSL","text":"HSL stands for hue, saturation and lightness. It's the easiest format to adjust by hand: keep the hue and change the lightness to get a darker or lighter shade of the same color. hue (the color wheel) saturation (gray → vivid) lightness (black → white) Named colors Aa on white Aa on black The same color, written three ways hex rgb hsl Contrast for text (needs 4.5 : 1 or more) Part 2"},{"id":"sec-units-how-big-is-12","title":"Units: how big is \"12\"?","text":"Each bar below is the same number with a different unit. Change the settings and watch which bars move. html font-size (sets rem) parent font-size (sets em) parent width (sets %)"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Hex like #2340d9 is pairs of red, green and blue, from 00 to ff. Add transparency with a fourth value: rgb(35 64 217 / 50%). Use rem for font sizes so text grows when someone sets a larger default size in their browser. Use %, fr or max-width for widths so layouts can shrink on phones. em depends on the parent, so nested em sizes multiply. That's why most people prefer rem."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Light gray text on white. It looks sleek and fails contrast, so many people can't read it. Forgetting the # on a hex color, or the unit on a size: width: 200 does nothing. Setting width: 100vw, which ignores the scrollbar and causes sideways scrolling. Using px for everything, so the page ignores the user's font size setting."}]},{"slug":"css-variables","num":"2.11","title":"CSS Variables","desc":"Store a value once and reuse it everywhere, including a dark theme.","props":["--brand","var()",":root"],"intro":"A CSS variable (custom property) stores a value once so you can reuse it everywhere. Change one variable here and every part of the site that uses it updates at once.","more":"Variables on #site --brand --accent --radius --space Try these too Dark theme (redefines --bg, --text, --card) Featured card overrides --brand Define --sale Pick a variable above to outline everything that uses it. Trailhead3 new trails Royal Arch 3.4 mi · Hard Guided hike: 20% off Save Mesa Trail 6.7 mi · Featured Save Chautauqua 1.2 mi · Easy Save","sections":[{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Define variables with two dashes, usually on :root so the whole page can use them: --brand: #0b6b5e; Use them with var(): color: var(--brand); Variables inherit. Redefine one on a single element, and only that element and its children see the new value. Give a fallback for when a variable isn't defined: var(--sale, crimson). A dark theme can be as simple as redefining a few color variables."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Forgetting var(): color: --brand; does nothing. Only one dash, or a typo in the name. Variable names are case-sensitive. Doing math without calc(): use calc(var(--space) * 2). Defining the variable on an element that isn't a parent of where you use it."}]},{"slug":"backgrounds-borders","num":"2.12","title":"Backgrounds & Borders","desc":"Gradients, background images, layering, borders and rounded corners.","props":["linear-gradient","background-size","border-radius"],"intro":"Backgrounds can be a color, a gradient, an image, or several layered together. Borders frame a box and border-radius rounds its corners. Build a trail card from the pieces.","more":"Background type solid color linear-gradient radial-gradient image image + gradient overlay repeating image color 1 color 2 gradient angle background-size cover contain auto 50% background-position center top bottom left right background-repeat no-repeat repeat repeat-x Border border-width border-style solid dashed dotted double groove border-color border-radius Only round the top-left and bottom-right corners Mesa Trail 6.7 miles · Moderate","sections":[{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Gradients are images, so they go in background-image (or the background shorthand), not background-color. Layer backgrounds with commas. The first one listed is drawn on top. A dark gradient over a photo keeps white text readable. border-radius: 50% on a square makes a circle. Background image paths in CSS start from the CSS file's folder."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"A background image that never shows because the element has no height. Using a background image for content that matters. Screen readers can't see it and it has no alt text. White text on a busy photo with no overlay. Setting border-width without border-style. The default style is none, so nothing appears."}]},{"slug":"shadows","num":"2.13","title":"Shadows & Filters","desc":"box-shadow, text-shadow on photos, drop-shadow, and other filters.","props":["box-shadow","text-shadow","filter"],"intro":"Shadows make flat boxes look lifted off the page, and filters change how an element is drawn, like a photo editor. A little goes a long way: soft, subtle shadows look the most natural.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-box-shadow","title":"box-shadow","text":"A shadow has an x offset, a y offset, a blur, a spread and a color. Try the presets, then adjust them yourself. x offset y offset blur spread color opacity inset (shadow inside the box) Add a second, tighter shadow layer Mesa Trail 6.7 miles · Moderate · Dogs on leash Part 2"},{"id":"sec-text-shadow-for-readable-text-on-images","title":"text-shadow for readable text on images","text":"blur shadow darkness Busy background Above Treeline Part 3"},{"id":"sec-box-shadow-vs-filter-drop-shadow","title":"box-shadow vs filter: drop-shadow()","text":"box-shadow always follows the element's rectangle. The drop-shadow filter follows the actual shape, including transparent parts of an image. box-shadow The shadow outlines the image's square box. filter: drop-shadow() The shadow traces the mountain's shape. Part 4"},{"id":"sec-other-filters","title":"Other filters","text":"filter none grayscale(1) sepia(1) blur(4px) brightness(0.6) contrast(1.6) saturate(2) hue-rotate(120deg) A common use: show photos in grayscale and bring the color back on hover."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Use a semi-transparent shadow color like rgb(0 0 0 / 15%). Solid black looks harsh. Shadows coming slightly down (positive y) look natural, like light from above. Stack shadows with commas: a tight dark one plus a wide soft one looks realistic. Shadows don't take up space or move other elements. They're painted on top."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Heavy black shadows on everything, so nothing stands out. Relying on a shadow alone to separate a card from a similar background. Add a border too. Text shadows on body text. Save them for big text on images. Animating box-shadow on many elements, which can make scrolling choppy."}]},{"slug":"typography","num":"2.14","title":"Typography","desc":"Fonts, sizes, line height, and making text easy to read.","props":["font-family","line-height"],"intro":"Most of a web page is text, so how it reads matters more than almost anything else. Adjust the settings and watch the readability checks respond.","more":"font-family Atkinson Hyperlegible Lora (serif) Georgia (serif) system-ui (your OS font) Arial Times New Roman monospace font-size line-height max-width (line length) font-weight 300 400 700 letter-spacing text-align left center justify right Why the trail closes in spring Every April, the lower section of the Mesa Trail closes for about six weeks. Snowmelt turns the clay soil into deep mud, and hikers who step around the puddles widen the trail and trample the wildflowers growing at its edges. Rangers reopen the trail once the ground can hold a footprint without sinking. Until then, the upper loop stays open and drains much faster. Type scale","sections":[{"id":"sec-heading-sizes-that-belong-together","title":"Heading sizes that belong together","text":"Pick a base size and a ratio. Each heading level is the one below it multiplied by the ratio, so the sizes feel related instead of random. base size ratio 1.125 (subtle) 1.25 (balanced) 1.333 (clear) 1.5 (bold) 1.618 (dramatic)"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Aim for 45 to 75 characters per line. Set max-width in ch on paragraphs. Body text reads best with a line-height between about 1.4 and 1.8, and no unit. Always end a font-family list with a generic fallback like sans-serif or serif. Use two typefaces at most: one for headings, one for body text."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Lines stretching the full width of a big monitor, so readers lose their place. Centering long paragraphs. Center short headings only. Body text under 16px, which is hard to read on phones. Font names with spaces but no quotes, or a Google Font used without its <link> tag."}]},{"slug":"web-fonts","num":"2.15","title":"Web Fonts","desc":"Loading fonts from Google Fonts, fallbacks, and what happens while they load.","props":["@font-face","font-display","fallback"],"intro":"Visitors only have a handful of fonts installed. A web font is downloaded along with your page so everyone sees the same type. Here's how to add one, what happens while it loads, and why the fallback still matters.","more":"The easy way: Google Fonts Find a font at fonts.google.com and choose the weights you need. Copy the <link> tags it gives you into your page's <head>, above your own stylesheet. Use the font in CSS with font-family, followed by fallbacks. Self-hosting with @font-face Put the .woff2 files in a fonts/ folder in your project. Add an @font-face rule naming the font and pointing to the file. Use that name in font-family like any other font. Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-always-include-a-fallback","title":"Always include a fallback","text":"If the font fails to load, or hasn't loaded yet, the browser uses the next font in your list. Pick one that's similar. web font Merriweather Playfair Display Roboto Slab Source Sans 3 Space Mono Pacifico fallback serif sans-serif monospace cursive Pretend the font failed to load Weekend Hikes Near Boulder Pack water, layers and a map. Afternoon storms roll in fast above treeline, so start early and turn back by noon. Part 2"},{"id":"sec-what-visitors-see-while-it-loads","title":"What visitors see while it loads","text":"font-display decides what happens during the download. Pick a setting and simulate a slow connection where the font takes 2.5 seconds. font-display swap block fallback optional Simulate slow load Royal Arch Trail 0sfont arrives at 2.5s3s Press \"Simulate slow load.\" Part 3"},{"id":"sec-only-load-what-you-use","title":"Only load what you use","text":"Every weight and style is a separate file to download. These sizes are rough estimates for a typical font. Extra download"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Put font names with spaces in quotes: font-family: \"Source Sans 3\", sans-serif; End every font list with a generic family: serif, sans-serif or monospace. font-display: swap is a good default. Text shows right away and switches when the font arrives. Two families and three or four weights is plenty for most sites."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Using a font name in CSS without the <link> or @font-face that loads it. It only works on computers that happen to have it installed. Asking for font-weight: 700 when only 400 was downloaded, so the browser fakes a blurry bold. Misspelling the font name. CSS silently falls back with no error. Fancy script fonts for body text, which are hard to read at small sizes."}]},{"slug":"flexbox","num":"3.1","title":"Flexbox","desc":"Line items up in a row or column, then space and align them.","props":["display: flex","justify-content","align-items","gap"],"intro":"Flexbox lays out items in one direction, a row or a column. You set the rules on the container, and its direct children (the flex items) follow them.","more":".container flex-direction row row-reverse column column-reverse justify-content flex-start center flex-end space-between space-around space-evenly align-items stretch center flex-start flex-end baseline flex-wrap nowrap wrap gap 12px Items number of items 4 .item:first-child { flex-grow } 0 (don't grow) 1 (fill extra space) Practice","sections":[{"id":"sec-build-a-nav-bar","title":"Build a nav bar","text":"Most site headers are a flex row inside a flex row. Try it on a real nav, then press Check my work."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"justify-content works along the main axis, which is the direction set by flex-direction. align-items works along the cross axis, perpendicular to the main axis. Switch to column and the two swap: now justify-content moves things up and down. gap adds space between items without margins on each one."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Putting display: flex on the items instead of their parent. Expecting align-items: center to center vertically when the container has no extra height. Forgetting flex-wrap: wrap, so items squish instead of moving to a new line. Using Flexbox for a full two-dimensional grid. Reach for CSS Grid instead."}]},{"slug":"grid","num":"3.2","title":"CSS Grid","desc":"Rows and columns at the same time, with items that span cells.","props":["grid-template-columns","fr","span"],"intro":"Grid controls rows and columns at the same time. Define the columns on the container, and items fill the cells in order unless you tell one to span more space.","more":".container grid-template-columns repeat(3, 1fr) repeat(4, 1fr) 1fr 2fr 1fr 200px 1fr repeat(auto-fit, minmax(120px, 1fr)) gap 12px justify-items stretch start center end align-items stretch start center end Items number of items 7 .item-1 { grid-column } span 1 span 2 span 3 .item-1 { grid-row } span 1 span 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"fr means \"a fraction of the leftover space.\" 1fr 2fr makes the second column twice as wide. repeat(3, 1fr) is shorthand for 1fr 1fr 1fr. grid-column: span 2 makes an item take up two columns. Other items flow around it. repeat(auto-fit, minmax(120px, 1fr)) makes a responsive grid with no media queries. Resize the window to see it."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Setting grid-template-columns on the items instead of the container. Spanning more columns than the grid has, which creates extra implicit columns. Using fixed px columns everywhere, so the layout overflows on phones. Mixing up justify-items (horizontal, inside each cell) with justify-content (the whole grid)."}]},{"slug":"flex-vs-grid","num":"3.3","title":"Flexbox vs Grid","desc":"Six real layouts built both ways, and which one fits each job.","props":["flex","grid","when to use"],"intro":"Both can build most layouts, but each has a sweet spot. Flexbox arranges items along one line and lets their content decide their size. Grid lays out rows and columns together. Pick a layout and compare.","more":"container width Flexbox Grid Rule of thumb Content out: if the items' sizes should decide the layout (a row of buttons, tags, a navbar), use Flexbox. Layout in: if you want to draw the rows and columns first and drop items into them (a gallery, a page, a dashboard), use Grid. Real sites use both, often nested.","sections":[{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Flexbox is one-dimensional: a row or a column. Wrapped lines don't line up with each other. Grid is two-dimensional: columns line up from row to row. Only Grid can make one item span several rows and columns. It's normal to put a flex container inside a grid cell, or a grid inside a flex item."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Building a card gallery with Flexbox and getting a stretched last row. Adding lots of wrapper divs to fake a two-dimensional layout with Flexbox. Using Grid for a simple row of buttons and fighting fixed column widths. Thinking one is \"better.\" They solve different problems."}]},{"slug":"floats","num":"3.4","title":"Floats","desc":"Wrap text around an image, clear floats, and fix collapsing parents.","props":["float","clear","flow-root"],"intro":"A floated element moves to the left or right, and the text flows around it, like a photo in a magazine. That's still the job floats do best. Before Flexbox and Grid existed they were also used for whole-page layouts, so you'll see them in older code.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-wrap-text-around-an-image","title":"Wrap text around an image","text":"float none left right margin (space around it) Add clear: both to the next heading Round photo with shape-outside: circle() photo Royal Arch is one of the most rewarding short hikes near Boulder. The trail climbs steeply through ponderosa pine forest, gaining about 1,400 feet in under two miles. Getting there Park at Chautauqua and follow the Bluebell Road to the trailhead. The final stretch has over 200 stone steps, so take your time and save some energy for the way down. Part 2"},{"id":"sec-the-collapsing-parent","title":"The collapsing parent","text":"A floated child no longer counts toward its parent's height. If everything inside is floated, the parent shrinks to almost nothing and the next element slides up underneath. Fix the parent with display: flow-root parent (dashed border) floated The next section"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"A floated element needs a width, or it shrinks to fit its content. Give floats a margin on the side facing the text so words don't touch the image. clear: both makes an element start below any floats above it. display: flow-root on the parent makes it contain its floats. Older code used a \"clearfix\" hack for this. For side-by-side layout today, use Flexbox or Grid. Use floats for wrapping text."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Building columns with floats, then fighting collapsed parents and uneven heights. Forgetting that floats don't work on flex or grid items. The float is ignored. On phones, a floated image squeezes the text into a narrow strip. Use a media query to remove the float on small screens. Floating something to push it right, when margin-left: auto or justify-content would be simpler."}]},{"slug":"positioning","num":"3.5","title":"Positioning","desc":"relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky, and what each one is relative to.","props":["position","top","z-index"],"intro":"The position property decides what top and left are measured from, and whether the element still takes up space. Change the settings, then scroll inside the window to compare.","more":"#box position static relative absolute fixed sticky top left z-index .parent position: relative browser window .parent sibling 1 #box sibling 2 · z-index 1 Scroll inside the window above. The window has its own scrollbar.","sections":[{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"static is the default. top and left do nothing. relative nudges the element from where it would have been. Its original space stays reserved. absolute removes the element from the flow and places it inside the nearest positioned ancestor. That's why you put position: relative on the parent. fixed places it relative to the browser window, so it stays put while you scroll. sticky scrolls normally until it reaches the top value, then sticks until its parent scrolls away."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Using absolute without a positioned parent, so the element flies to the corner of the page. Building whole layouts with absolute positioning. Use Flexbox or Grid, and save positioning for overlays and badges. Setting z-index on a static element. It only works on positioned (or flex and grid) items. Sticky that never sticks because a parent has overflow: hidden, or no top value is set."}]},{"slug":"media-queries","num":"4.1","title":"Media Queries","desc":"Change a layout at specific screen widths with breakpoints.","props":["@media","min-width","breakpoints"],"intro":"A media query applies CSS only when a condition is true, usually \"the screen is at least this wide.\" Drag the slider to change the pretend screen width and watch which rules switch on.","more":"Screen width 375px Phone · 375 Tablet · 768 Laptop · 1280 Trailhead TrailsMapsGear Weekend hikes near you Royal Arch3.4 mi · Hard Mesa Trail6.7 mi · Moderate Chautauqua Loop1.2 mi · Easy Trail conditions Muddy below 7,000 ft. Bring layers.","sections":[{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Mobile first: write the phone layout with no media query, then add min-width queries as the screen gets bigger. @media (min-width: 600px) means \"600px or wider.\" Both queries are on at 1000px. Later rules win when they conflict, so list queries from smallest to largest. Pick breakpoints where your layout starts to look wrong, not by device names."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Forgetting <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"> in the <head>. Phones will ignore your queries without it. Missing the space or parentheses: @media(min-width:600px) is fine, but @media min-width: 600px is not. Putting the media query above the base rules, so the base rules override it. Mixing min-width and max-width queries until nobody can tell which applies."}]},{"slug":"responsive-images","num":"4.2","title":"Responsive Images","desc":"Images that shrink to fit and never overflow their container.","props":["max-width","srcset","object-fit"],"intro":"Images have a fixed size, but screens don't. Here's how to stop images from overflowing, crop them to fit a shape, and send phones a smaller file.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-make-it-shrink","title":"Make it shrink","text":"This photo is 800 × 500 pixels. Make the container narrower and see what each setting does. container width max-width: 100% height: auto The img tag has width=\"800\" height=\"500\" attributes, which you should always include. Part 2"},{"id":"sec-fit-it-to-a-shape-with-object-fit","title":"Fit it to a shape with object-fit","text":"When an image has to fill a box of a different shape, like a square profile photo or a wide banner, object-fit decides whether to stretch, shrink or crop it. box shape square tall wide object-fit fill contain cover none scale-down object-position center left right top bottom Part 3"},{"id":"sec-send-the-right-file-with-srcset","title":"Send the right file with srcset","text":"With srcset you list several copies of the same image at different widths. The browser downloads the smallest one that still looks sharp on the visitor's screen. image shows at (CSS px) screen pixel density 1x 2x 3x Most phones are 2x or 3x: each CSS pixel uses 2 or 3 real pixels across."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Put max-width: 100% and height: auto on images in nearly every stylesheet. Keep the width and height attributes in the HTML. The browser uses them to reserve space before the image loads. object-fit: cover fills the box and crops the edges. contain shows the whole image and leaves empty space. Use object-position to keep the important part of the photo in view when it's cropped."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Uploading a 4000px photo straight from a phone. It can be 5 MB for something shown 400px wide. Setting both width and height in CSS without object-fit, which stretches the image. Using width: 100% on a small image, which blows it up and makes it blurry. Listing srcset widths that don't match the real files' widths."}]},{"slug":"picture","num":"4.3","title":"Picture & Lazy Loading","desc":"The sizes attribute, different crops with picture, and loading images only when needed.","props":["sizes","<picture>","loading=\"lazy\""],"intro":"Three more ways to send the right image: tell the browser how wide it will be with sizes, swap in a different crop with <picture>, and skip images nobody scrolls to with loading=\"lazy\".","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-tell-the-browser-the-size-with-sizes","title":"Tell the browser the size with sizes","text":"The browser picks a file from srcset before your CSS has loaded, so it doesn't know how wide the image will be. Without sizes, it assumes the full window width. This layout shows 1, 2 or 3 cards per row. window width sizes (no sizes attribute) 100vw (min-width: 700px) 50vw, 100vw (min-width: 1100px) 33vw, (min-width: 700px) 50vw, 100vw screen pixel density 1x 2x Read sizes left to right: the first media condition that's true wins, and the last value has no condition, so it's the default. Part 2"},{"id":"sec-change-the-crop-with-picture","title":"Change the crop with <picture>","text":"Sometimes a smaller copy isn't enough. A wide landscape shrunk onto a phone makes the arch a speck. <picture> lets you send a different crop at different widths. This is called art direction. window width The browser checks each <source> in order and uses the first one whose media matches. If none match, it uses the <img>. The <img> is required. It holds the alt text, and any CSS you write targets the img, not the picture. Part 3"},{"id":"sec-skip-what-nobody-sees-with-loading-lazy","title":"Skip what nobody sees with loading=\"lazy\"","text":"Scroll the page on the left and watch the network log. With lazy loading, images download just before they scroll into view. loading eager (default) lazy The first image is above the fold, so it is always loaded right away. Lazy-loading it would only make the page feel slower. Reload the page trailclub.github.io/hikes/ Downloaded so far"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Use srcset with w widths (trail-800.jpg 800w) plus sizes when the image isn't full-width. Use 1x/2x only for images with a fixed size, like a logo. sizes describes your layout. If your CSS changes the columns, update sizes to match. <picture> with media changes the crop. With type it offers a newer format and falls back: <source type=\"image/avif\" srcset=\"arch.avif\">. Put loading=\"lazy\" on images and iframes further down the page, and keep width and height so the page doesn't jump when they arrive."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Leaving out sizes with w descriptors, so a small card image downloads at full-window size. Using <picture> only to switch sizes. srcset and sizes on a plain <img> already do that, and let the browser choose. Forgetting the <img> inside <picture>. Without it, nothing shows. Lazy-loading the big photo at the top of the page. It should load as early as possible."}]},{"slug":"mobile-first","num":"4.4","title":"Mobile-First Workflow","desc":"Write the phone layout first, then add complexity as screens grow.","props":["min-width","viewport meta"],"intro":"Build the phone version first, then add to it as the screen gets wider. Step through how one page comes together and watch it on three screen sizes at once.","more":"Phone · 375px Tablet · 768px Laptop · 1280px How it looks now ← Previous step Next step → Before you hand it in","sections":[{"id":"sec-responsive-checklist","title":"Responsive checklist","text":"Tick these off for your own project. Your checks are saved in this browser only."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"The phone layout is usually the simplest: one column. Starting there means less CSS to undo later. Styles outside any media query apply everywhere. Media queries only add changes for wider screens. Use min-width queries, listed from smallest to largest. Add a breakpoint when the layout starts to look wrong as you widen the window, not at a specific device's width."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Designing for a laptop, then cramming it onto a phone with lots of max-width overrides. Only testing by shrinking the browser window. Check on a real phone too. Fixed widths like width: 960px on a wrapper, which cause sideways scrolling on phones. Hiding important content on mobile instead of rearranging it."}]},{"slug":"fluid-sizing","num":"4.5","title":"Fluid Sizing","desc":"Sizes that scale smoothly between limits with clamp(), min(), max() and calc().","props":["clamp()","min()","calc()"],"intro":"Media queries change things in jumps. clamp(), min(), max() and calc() let sizes change smoothly with the screen, inside limits you set, often with no media query at all.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-headings-that-grow-with-clamp","title":"Headings that grow with clamp()","text":"clamp(MIN, PREFERRED, MAX) uses the preferred value, but never goes below the minimum or above the maximum. Drag the window width and compare three ways to size a heading. window width clamp( min, preferred, max ) min preferred: 1rem + ?vw max 1rem is 16px here. Keeping a rem in the preferred value means the heading still grows when someone zooms in. fixed remvw onlyclamp() Part 2"},{"id":"sec-columns-that-fit-with-min-and-max","title":"Columns that fit with min() and max()","text":"min() picks the smallest of its values, max() the largest. They're perfect for \"this wide, but never wider than the screen\" and \"at least this much padding.\" page width .column { … } width: 640px max-width: 640px width: min(640px, 100% - 32px) padding 10px max(10px, 4vw) Part 3"},{"id":"sec-recipes-worth-keeping","title":"Recipes worth keeping","text":"Copy these into your own stylesheet. Each one replaces a media query or two."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3.5rem) means: grow with the window, but stay between 28px and 56px. calc() mixes units: calc(100% - 2rem) is \"full width, minus 32px.\" Inside clamp(), min() and max() you can do math without writing calc. min() sets an upper limit (like max-width), max() sets a lower limit (like min-width). The names feel backwards at first. These work anywhere a length works: font sizes, widths, padding, gap and grid tracks."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Leaving out the spaces: calc(100%-2rem) is ignored. The + and - need a space on each side. Sizing text in vw alone. It's tiny on phones, huge on big monitors, and doesn't grow when people zoom. Putting the values in the wrong order. clamp() is always min, preferred, max. Using vw for widths inside a page that has a scrollbar. 100vw includes the scrollbar, so it causes sideways scrolling. Use 100%."}]},{"slug":"transforms","num":"5.1","title":"Transforms","desc":"Move, rotate, scale and skew elements without disturbing the layout around them.","props":["translate","rotate","transform-origin"],"intro":"transform moves, turns, resizes or slants an element after the page is laid out. The element looks different, but the space it takes up doesn't change, so nothing around it moves.","more":"#box { transform } translateX translateY rotate scale skewX transform-origin center top left top right bottom left bottom center Reset sibling #box sibling Copy Order matters","sections":[{"id":"sec-same-functions-different-order","title":"Same functions, different order","text":"Transform functions run from right to left, and each one changes the direction the next one uses. Rotate first and \"move right\" points somewhere else. transform: translateX(140px) rotate(45deg); A Turn in place, then slide 140px right along the page. transform: rotate(45deg) translateX(140px); B Turn first, so \"right\" is now tilted 45° downward, and it slides along that tilted line. Play both In real sites"},{"id":"sec-four-everyday-uses","title":"Four everyday uses","text":"Center anything centered .badge { position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%); } top/left: 50% puts the corner in the middle. The translate pulls it back by half its own size. Lift on hover Hover or Tab to me .card { transition: transform 0.2s; } .card:hover { transform: translateY(-6px); } Turn an arrow Trail conditions Icy above 7,000 feet. details[open] summary::before { transform: rotate(90deg); } Mirror an icon ➜➜ .icon-back { transform: scaleX(-1); } One arrow icon serves as both \"next\" and \"back.\""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Transforms don't affect layout. Neighbors stay where they were, which is why they're perfect for hover effects and animation. List several functions in one transform, separated by spaces. A second transform declaration replaces the first entirely. transform-origin is the pin the element spins and scales around. The default is the center. translate percentages are a percentage of the element's own size, not its parent's. Transforms are smooth to animate. See Transitions & Animations."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Writing transform: rotate(10deg); and later transform: scale(1.1); on hover, and losing the rotation. Repeat both: rotate(10deg) scale(1.1). Transforming an inline element like a span. It's ignored: make it inline-block. Forgetting the unit: rotate(45) does nothing. Write 45deg. Using translate to build a layout. The original space stays reserved, so you get gaps and overlaps."}]},{"slug":"transitions","num":"5.2","title":"Transitions & Animations","desc":"Smooth state changes and keyframe animations, with timing curves.","props":["transition","@keyframes","ease"],"intro":"A transition smooths the change between two states, like on hover. An animation runs a sequence of keyframes by itself. Small, quick motion helps people notice changes; too much distracts.","more":"Your device is set to reduce motion. This page plays its demos anyway so you can learn from them, but the rest of this site turns motion off for you. Your own sites should do the same with a prefers-reduced-motion media query. Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-transitions","title":"Transitions","text":"what changes position (left) color and shape size rotation (transform) transition-duration transition-timing-function ease linear ease-in ease-out ease-in-out back (overshoot) steps(5) transition-delay Change state Speed curve Same timing on hover Hover or Tab to me Part 2"},{"id":"sec-keyframe-animations","title":"Keyframe animations","text":"animation-name pulse spin slide-in shake bounce animation-duration animation-iteration-count 1 3 infinite animation-direction normal alternate reverse Play animation box"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Put transition on the element's normal state, not the :hover state, so it animates both ways. Most interface motion should take 150–400ms. Longer feels sluggish. transform and opacity animate smoothly. Animating width, top or left makes the browser redo layout every frame. Wrap big motion in @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) so people who get motion sick are spared."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Trying to transition display: none. It can't fade; use opacity instead. Writing transition: all and animating things you didn't mean to. Forgetting the unit: transition-duration: 300 does nothing. Write 300ms or 0.3s. Infinite animations on content people are trying to read."}]},{"slug":"form-styling","num":"5.3","title":"Styling Forms","desc":"Turn a default form into a polished one, one layer at a time.","props":["font: inherit","accent-color",":user-invalid"],"intro":"Browser-default forms look cramped and inconsistent. A handful of rules fixes most of it. Add them one layer at a time and watch the same form improve.","more":"Name Please enter your name. Email That doesn't look like an email address. Favorite trail Royal Arch Mesa Trail Chautauqua Loop Message Optional. Tell us about your last hike. Email me when new trails are added Join the club","sections":[{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Inputs, selects and buttons don't inherit fonts. Start every form with font: inherit. Put labels above inputs. It's easiest to scan and works on narrow screens. accent-color recolors checkboxes, radios and range sliders in one line. :user-invalid only shows errors after someone has interacted with a field, not as soon as the page loads."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Removing the focus outline and replacing it with nothing. width: 100% plus padding without box-sizing: border-box, so inputs poke out of their container. Showing errors in red only. Add a text message too, for people who can't tell the colors apart. Tiny checkboxes and buttons that are hard to tap on a phone."}]},{"slug":"accessibility","num":"5.4","title":"Accessibility Basics","desc":"Seven common problems on one page, and how to find and fix them.","props":["keyboard","focus","contrast"],"intro":"An accessible site works for people using a keyboard, a screen reader, zoom, or a phone in bright sun. This sample page has seven common problems. Click into it and press Tab to move through it, then compare it with the fixed version.","more":"Broken version Fixed version Keyboard log: what Tab reaches Clear Click the sample page, then press Tab. Your turn","sections":[{"id":"sec-five-checks-for-your-own-project","title":"Five checks for your own project","text":"Unplug your mouseUse Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter and Space. Can you reach and use everything? Can you always see where you are? Zoom to 200%Press Ctrl or Cmd and + a few times. Nothing should overlap or get cut off. Check contrastBody text needs at least 4.5 : 1 against its background. The Colors & Units page can check it. Read the headings aloneDo h1, h2 and h3 make a sensible outline of the page, in order? Turn images offDoes the alt text tell you what you'd miss? See the Images & Alt Text page."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Use the right HTML element first. Real buttons, links, labels and headings come with accessibility built in. Everything you can do with a mouse should work with a keyboard. Every control needs a name a screen reader can say: label text, button text or alt text. Don't use color alone to show meaning, like red for errors. Add text or an icon."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Clickable divs and spans. They can't be reached with Tab. outline: none with no replacement focus style. Placeholder text used as the only label. Pale gray text because it \"looks clean.\""}]},{"slug":"organizing-css","num":"5.5","title":"Organizing CSS","desc":"Sections, comments, purposeful class names and BEM, and variables.","props":["comments","BEM","naming"],"intro":"A stylesheet that works today can still be a nightmare to change next week. Watch one messy stylesheet get cleaned up in four steps. It styles exactly the same page at every step; only how easy it is to work with changes.","more":"Practice","sections":[{"id":"sec-where-does-this-rule-go","title":"Where does this rule go?","text":"A common order for a stylesheet: base styles for plain elements, then layout, then components, then small utility classes. Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-better-class-name","title":"Pick the better class name","text":"Name classes for what something is, not how it looks. Looks change; purpose rarely does."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Group rules into sections with comment headings, always in the same order. Name classes after their purpose: .trail-card, not .blue-box. BEM is a popular naming pattern: .block, .block__element, .block--variation, like .card__title and .btn--large. Any color or size you type more than twice should probably be a CSS variable. Prefer classes over IDs and long chains of selectors. Low, even specificity is easier to override."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Adding every new rule to the bottom of the file. Names like .box2, .new or .left that stop making sense later. The same rule written twice in different places, with slightly different values. Leaving old, unused rules in \"just in case.\" Delete them; that's what version control is for."}]},{"slug":"devtools","num":"5.6","title":"Debugging with DevTools","desc":"Read the Styles pane, spot crossed-out rules, and fix three real bugs.","props":["Inspect","Styles","box model"],"intro":"Every browser has developer tools that show exactly which CSS rules apply to an element and which ones lost. This practice version works like the real Elements panel. Use it to fix three bugs on the trail card.","more":"localhost/trail.html Royal Arch 3.4 miles · Hard · 1,400 ft of gain to a sandstone arch above Boulder. Save trail ElementsConsoleSourcesNetwork The real thing","sections":[{"id":"sec-opening-devtools-in-your-browser","title":"Opening DevTools in your browser","text":"Inspect one elementRight-click it on the page and choose Inspect. DevTools opens with that element selected. Open DevToolsF12 or Ctrl+Shift+I on Windows. Cmd+Option+I on a Mac. In Safari, turn on \"Show features for web developers\" in Settings first. Test on a phone sizeClick the device toolbar icon (a phone and tablet) at the top of DevTools to preview any screen width. Changes aren't savedEdits in DevTools disappear when you reload. Once you've found the fix, copy it into your CSS file."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"The Styles pane lists every rule that matches, with the winning ones at the top. A crossed-out declaration lost to a stronger or later rule. Look above it to find the winner. The file name and line number next to each rule tell you exactly where to fix it. \"user agent stylesheet\" means the browser's built-in defaults. You can't edit them, but your own CSS can override them. The box model diagram shows an element's real margin, border, padding and size."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Fixing the problem in DevTools and forgetting to copy the fix into the real file. Searching your CSS for a rule that doesn't exist, when it's the browser's default. Not noticing a rule is missing completely, which usually means a typo in the selector or a stylesheet that didn't load. Debugging the wrong element. Check which element is selected in the tree."}]},{"slug":"validation","num":"5.7","title":"Validating Your Code","desc":"Use the W3C validators, spot common errors, and see how browsers repair bad HTML.","props":["W3C","errors","Elements panel"],"intro":"Browsers quietly guess their way around mistakes, so broken code often looks fine until it suddenly doesn't. A validator reads your code like a strict teacher and lists every problem with a line number.","more":"HTML W3C Markup Validator Choose \"Validate by Direct Input.\" Paste your whole HTML file. Click Check. CSS W3C CSS Validator Choose \"By direct input.\" Paste your stylesheet. Click Check. How to read the results Fix the first error first. One missing bracket can cause ten more errors below it. Go to the line number it gives, then look at the line just above it too. Check again after each fix. Practice","sections":[{"id":"sec-spot-the-error","title":"Spot the error","text":"Each snippet has exactly one line with a mistake. Click the line you think is wrong. HTML CSS Debugging HTML"},{"id":"sec-what-the-browser-actually-built","title":"What the browser actually built","text":"When HTML is broken, the browser silently repairs it, sometimes in surprising ways. DevTools' Elements panel shows the repaired version, not your file. Pick a snippet: the right side is your own browser's real result. Your HTML file What the Elements panel shows To see this in your own project Right-click the problem area and choose Inspect. In the Elements panel, compare the tree to your file. Wherever they differ, that's where your HTML has an error."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Validate before you hand in work, and whenever something looks wrong and you can't see why. \"Errors\" must be fixed. \"Warnings\" are suggestions worth reading. Indenting your code consistently makes missing closing tags and braces much easier to spot. Your code editor's highlighting is a validator too: if colors suddenly look wrong partway down, the mistake is just above."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Fixing errors from the bottom of the list up. Assuming code is fine because it looks right in one browser. Validating a single section instead of the whole file, which creates fake errors about missing tags. Ignoring the CSS validator because \"CSS just skips bad lines.\" That skipping is exactly the bug."}]},{"slug":"publishing","num":"5.8","title":"Project Structure & Publishing","desc":"Name and organize files, then publish with GitHub Pages.","props":["index.html","folders","GitHub Pages"],"intro":"A tidy folder and sensible file names prevent most \"it works on my laptop\" bugs. Clean up this messy project, then follow the steps to put a site online with GitHub Pages.","more":"Part 1","sections":[{"id":"sec-clean-up-a-messy-project","title":"Clean up a messy project","text":"Pick the best new name for each file. The folder on the right updates as you go. Naming rules My Page.htmlmy-page.html Photo.JPGphoto.jpg final (2).cssstyle.css IMG_2041.jpgroyal-arch.jpg Lowercase, dashes instead of spaces, no special characters, and names that say what's inside. Part 2"},{"id":"sec-publish-with-github-pages","title":"Publish with GitHub Pages","text":"GitHub Pages hosts plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript sites for free. Make a GitHub accountPick your username carefully. It becomes part of your site's address. Create a new repositoryClick New, give it a short lowercase name like trail-site, set it to Public, and create it. Upload your filesClick \"Add file\" then \"Upload files.\" Drag in the contents of your project folder so index.html sits at the top level, then click Commit changes. Turn on PagesGo to Settings, then Pages. Under \"Build and deployment,\" choose \"Deploy from a branch,\" pick main and / (root), and click Save. Wait, then visitIt takes a minute or two. Refresh the Pages settings to see your link. After each change you upload, give it another minute. Where will my site be? GitHub username Repository name If it doesn't work I get a 404 page Make sure there's a file named exactly index.html (all lowercase) at the top of the repository, not inside another folder. Then wait a couple of minutes and refresh. The page loads but has no styles or images Check your paths. Capitalization must match exactly, and paths starting with / point to the wrong place on project sites. Use relative paths like css/style.css. My changes don't show up Wait a minute after committing, then do a hard refresh (Ctrl+Shift+R, or Cmd+Shift+R on a Mac) so your browser doesn't show an old cached copy. It shows my README instead of my site GitHub Pages couldn't find index.html, so it fell back to the README. Check the file name and that it's at the top level."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"The home page is always named index.html. Servers open it automatically. Group files by type: css/, images/, and subfolders for big sections. Keep one project in one folder, and open that folder in your code editor. Test the published site, not just your local copy. Servers are stricter about file names."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Uploading the project folder itself, so the site ends up at /my-project/index.html one level too deep. Image paths that point to your Desktop or Downloads folder. Two copies of a file, like style.css and style (1).css, and editing the wrong one. Making the repository private on a free account, which turns Pages off."}]},{"slug":"js-intro","num":"6.1","title":"What Is JavaScript?","desc":"What JavaScript adds to HTML and CSS, and where the script tag goes so your code can find the page.","props":["<script>","defer","type=\"module\""],"intro":"HTML gives a page its structure, CSS gives it style, and JavaScript gives it behavior. Turn each layer on and off to see what it adds, then find out where the <script> tag needs to go.","more":"Layers HTMLStructure: there is a button, and it says something. CSSPresentation: colors, font, border, shape. JavaScriptBehavior: clicking the button lets you change the name. Each layer builds on the one before it. JavaScript can't add behavior to a button that doesn't exist. Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-where-does-the-script-tag-go","title":"Where does the script tag go?","text":"A script often uses elements on the page. If it runs before the browser has read those elements, it finds nothing and fails. Pick a placement and see what happens as the page loads. Placement <script src> in <head> <script defer src> in <head> <script> at the end of <body> type=\"module\", opened from your computer type=\"module\", on a web server Practice"},{"id":"sec-check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"JavaScript is a programming language that runs in the browser. It can change content, react to clicks and typing, and fetch new data without reloading the page. Put JavaScript in its own file and link it: <script defer src=\"script.js\"></script>. Keeping HTML, CSS and JS in separate files keeps each one easy to read. defer downloads the script while the HTML loads, then runs it once the whole page has been read. type=\"module\" works the same way and is what MDN recommends, but it only runs on a web server (or a local server), not from a file you double-clicked. Comments: // one line or /* several lines */."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"A plain <script src> in the head with no defer: the elements don't exist yet, so you get null errors. Inline handlers like <button onclick=\"go()\">. MDN calls this bad practice. Use addEventListener in your JS file instead. Writing <script src=\"script.js\" />. A script tag always needs a closing </script>. A wrong path in src. Nothing happens, and the console shows a 404 for the file."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"What is JavaScript?The layer cake, adding scripts, and loading strategies. The <script> elementEvery attribute, including defer, async and type."}]},{"slug":"js-errors","num":"6.2","title":"The Console & Errors","desc":"Read error messages, find the line that's really broken, and debug with console.log().","props":["console.log()","TypeError","SyntaxError"],"intro":"When JavaScript breaks, the page usually just stops working with no message. The explanation is in the browser's console. This guessing game has five bugs: use the console to track them down.","more":"Test the game 1. Load the page 2. Guess 50 and submit 3. Play 4 guesses: 50, 25, 12, 6 4. Add console.log(randomNumber), reload 5 times Open the console in your own browser with F12, or ⌥⌘J on a Mac (Chrome). In Safari, turn on Show features for web developers first. Start over with all bugs Run a test, read the console, then click the line of code you think is broken. Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-error-message-decoder","title":"Error message decoder","text":"These are the errors you'll see most often in Chrome. Firefox and Safari word some of them differently, but the type at the start (SyntaxError, TypeError, ReferenceError) is the same everywhere. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-it-yourself","title":"Fix it yourself","text":"This code has three bugs. Run it, read the first error, fix that one, and run it again. When it works, the console prints three lines."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Syntax errors are typos in the grammar of the code: a missing bracket or quote. The whole script won't run. Runtime errors happen while the code runs, such as calling something that isn't a function. Code inside a function only fails when that function is called. Logic errors don't show any message. The code runs but does the wrong thing. Use console.log() to check values as the code runs. Every error message tells you what went wrong and where: game.js:21:13 means line 21, character 13. Fix the first error first. One error often stops the rest of the code from running, so later errors can't appear yet."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Assuming the error is on the line it names. That's where the code crashed. The real cause can be earlier, such as a selector that matched nothing. Wrong capitalization: addeventListener, queryselector, textcontent. JavaScript is case-sensitive. Using = (assign) when you mean === (compare) inside an if. Forgetting to reload the page after saving, then debugging the old code."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"What went wrong? Troubleshooting JavaScriptThe same guessing game, with its bugs explained step by step. JavaScript error referenceAn explanation for every error message."}]},{"slug":"js-variables","num":"6.3","title":"Variables & Data Types","desc":"let vs const, naming rules, and the difference between 42 and \"42\".","props":["let","const","typeof"],"intro":"A variable is a labeled box that holds a value. let boxes can be refilled; const boxes can't. Step through the code and watch the boxes change.","more":"◂ Back Run next line ▸ Restart Variables in memory Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-name-checker","title":"Name checker","text":"Some names break your code, and others just make it hard to read. Type a variable name, or try one of the examples. Variable name Try it"},{"id":"sec-typeof","title":"typeof","text":"typeof tells you what type a value is. Quotes always make a string, even when the value looks like a number. Change the values and run it again. Practice"},{"id":"sec-check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Use const when you can, and let when you have to. Use let only when the value needs to change, such as a score or a counter. Declare a variable once with let or const, then use just its name afterward. The main types: number (42, 3.4), string (\"text\"), boolean (true/false), plus arrays and objects for groups of values. JavaScript is dynamically typed: the value decides the type, and a let variable can hold a different type later. A const array or object can still have its contents changed. What can't change is which array or object the name refers to."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Using var. It's the old way, with confusing rules. MDN says there's no reason to use it in new code. Writing let again when updating a value: let score = 1; then let score = 2; is an error. Numbers in quotes: \"5\" + 5 gives \"55\", not 10. Mixing up case: myAge and myage are two different variables."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"Storing the information you need: Variableslet, const, naming rules and types. typeofEvery possible typeof result."}]},{"slug":"js-strings-numbers","num":"6.4","title":"Numbers & Strings","desc":"Math operators, why \"5\" + 5 is \"55\", template literals and string methods.","props":["+","`${ }`",".slice()"],"intro":"Math works the way you'd expect, until a number turns out to be text. Mix numbers and strings below to see when + adds and when it joins.","more":"a number \"string\" b number \"string\" Operator Values typed into a form field are always strings. Click \"string\" on one side to see what happens if you forget to convert them. Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-useful-string-methods","title":"Useful string methods","text":"Every character in a string has an index, starting at 0. Pick a method to see what it returns. Methods return a new value; the original string never changes. const text = Try it"},{"id":"sec-template-literals-and-math","title":"Template literals and Math","text":"Backticks let you put variables right inside a string with ${ }. It's easier to read than joining strings with +. Practice"},{"id":"sec-predict-the-output","title":"Predict the output","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Arithmetic: + - * /, % (remainder) and ** (power). Use parentheses to control the order. If either side of + is a string, it joins instead of adding. The other operators always try to convert to numbers. Convert text to a number with Number(\"12\"), and a number to text with String(12) or .toFixed(2). Shortcuts: score += 10, count++, count--. Template literals use backticks: `Hi ${name}`. They can also span several lines. .length is a property (no parentheses). .toUpperCase() is a method (with parentheses)."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Adding form values without converting them: \"2\" + \"3\" is \"23\". Using normal quotes for a template: \"Hi ${name}\" prints the ${name} literally. It needs backticks. Expecting a method to change the original: text.trim(); on its own does nothing useful. Save the result: text = text.trim(); Off-by-one indexes: the first character is [0], and the last is [text.length - 1]. Expecting 0.1 + 0.2 to be exactly 0.3. Round for display with .toFixed()."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"Basic math in JavaScriptOperators, precedence, and increment and decrement. Handling text: stringsQuotes, escaping, and template literals. Useful string methodslength, indexes, slice, replace and more."}]},{"slug":"js-arrays","num":"6.5","title":"Arrays","desc":"Lists of values with zero-based indexes, and the methods that add, remove and search.","props":["[0]",".push()",".length"],"intro":"An array stores a list of values under one name, in order, with a numbered index starting at 0. Some methods change the array, and others just hand you back an answer. Try them below.","more":"New item Add and remove Read and search Convert Reset array Try it","sections":[{"id":"sec-pick-something-at-random","title":"Pick something at random","text":"MDN's silly story challenge uses this pattern to choose a random item from an array. Math.random() gives a number from 0 up to 1. Multiply it by the length and round down to get a valid index. Practice"},{"id":"sec-predict-the-output","title":"Predict the output","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Create an array with square brackets: const trails = [\"Mesa\", \"Royal Arch\"]; Read an item by its index: trails[0] is the first item. The last one is trails[trails.length - 1]. Change an item: trails[1] = \"Flagstaff\"; push() and pop() work at the end; unshift() and shift() work at the start. splice() removes from the middle. Arrays can hold any type, including other arrays and objects. Convert between strings and arrays with split() and join()."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Starting to count at 1. trails[1] is the second item. Reading trails[trails.length]. It's one past the end, so you get undefined. Saving what push() returns and expecting the array: const list = trails.push(\"x\"); gives the new length. Thinking indexOf() returns false when nothing is found. It returns -1. Use includes() for a true/false answer."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"ArraysCreating, reading, adding and removing items. Challenge: Silly story generatorPut variables, strings and arrays together. Array referenceEvery array method."}]},{"slug":"js-conditionals","num":"6.6","title":"Conditionals","desc":"if, else if and else, comparing with ===, combining with && and ||, and truthy values.","props":["if / else","===","&&"],"intro":"An if statement lets code make decisions. JavaScript checks each condition from top to bottom, runs the first block that is true, and skips everything else.","more":"const temp = ; Put the conditions in the wrong order Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-and-or-not","title":"AND, OR, NOT","text":"Combine conditions with && (both must be true), || (at least one must be true) and ! (flips true and false). const isWeekend = ; const isRaining = ; Operator && || Part 3"},{"id":"sec-truthy-and-falsy","title":"Truthy and falsy","text":"A condition doesn't have to be a comparison. Any value works: six values count as false, and everything else counts as true. This is how if (name) checks whether someone typed anything. const value = Try it"},{"id":"sec-the-ternary-and-switch","title":"===, the ternary and switch","text":"Always compare with ===. The ternary condition ? a : b is a short if/else that produces a value. switch checks one value against many exact matches. Practice"},{"id":"sec-predict-the-output","title":"Predict the output","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"if (condition) { … } else if (other) { … } else { … }. Only the first block whose condition is true runs. Comparisons: === equal, !== not equal, <, >, <=, >=. Use ===, not ==. Triple equals also checks the type, so \"5\" === 5 is false. Order matters: put the most specific conditions first. Falsy values: false, 0, \"\", null, undefined, NaN. Everything else is truthy."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"if (x = 5): a single = assigns instead of comparing, so the condition is always true. if (day === \"Sat\" || \"Sun\"): each side needs its own comparison: day === \"Sat\" || day === \"Sun\". Putting temp < 60 before temp < 32. The second can never run. Forgetting break in a switch, so the code falls through into the next case. A semicolon after the condition, as in if (ok); {, makes the block always run."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"Making decisions in your code: conditionalsif...else, logical operators, switch and the ternary operator. FalsyThe full list of falsy values."}]},{"slug":"js-loops","num":"6.7","title":"Loops","desc":"Step through for, for...of and while loops, then let map() and filter() do the looping.","props":["for...of","for",".map()"],"intro":"A loop runs the same code again and again, usually once for each item in a list. Step through each kind of loop to see exactly what runs, and in what order.","more":"Loop for (classic) for...of while for with <= (bug) while without i++ (bug) Step ▸ Auto-play Restart Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-map-and-filter","title":"map() and filter()","text":"Arrays have methods that do the looping for you. You pass in a small function, and the method calls it once for each item. Try it"},{"id":"sec-break-and-continue","title":"break and continue","text":"break stops the loop completely. continue skips the rest of this pass and moves on to the next item. Practice"},{"id":"sec-add-up-the-miles","title":"Add up the miles","text":"Adding up a list is one of the most common jobs for a loop: start a total at 0, then add each item to it. Practice"},{"id":"sec-predict-the-output","title":"Predict the output","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"for...of is the simplest way to go through every item: for (const trail of trails) { … }. Use it when you don't need the index. A classic for loop has three parts: (initializer; condition; final-expression). Use it when you need to count, or need the index. while repeats as long as its condition is true. Good when you don't know in advance how many times to repeat. map() makes a new array of changed items. filter() makes a new array of only the items that pass a test. querySelectorAll() results work with for...of too. That's how you add a listener to every button on a page."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"i <= arr.length runs one time too many and reads undefined. Use <. Forgetting to change the counter in a while loop. The condition never becomes false, and the page freezes. Using const for a classic loop counter: for (const i = 0; …; i++) fails because i++ reassigns it. (const is fine in for...of.) Forgetting to return in a map() function that uses curly braces, which gives an array of undefined."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"Looping codefor...of, map, filter, for, while, break and continue. Array.prototype.map() and filter()Full reference."}]},{"slug":"js-functions","num":"6.8","title":"Functions","desc":"Parameters, arguments and return values, arrow functions, and where variables can be seen.","props":["function","return","=>"],"intro":"A function is a named, reusable block of code. You pass values in, it does some work, and it can hand a value back. Change the arguments and follow the values through the function.","more":"Arguments miles pace (leave empty to use the default) Common mistakes Forget the return Call it without () Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-three-ways-to-write-a-function","title":"Three ways to write a function","text":"All three do the same thing. You'll see all of them in code online. MDN recommends arrow functions for short functions, especially ones you pass to another function. Part 3"},{"id":"sec-scope-where-can-a-variable-be-seen","title":"Scope: where can a variable be seen?","text":"A variable declared inside a function only exists inside that function. One declared outside every function (global) can be used anywhere. Click a cell to try using that variable from that place. Click a cell. Try it"},{"id":"sec-build-your-own-function","title":"Build your own function","text":"Change the arguments, add a default, or write a new function that calls these. Practice"},{"id":"sec-predict-the-output","title":"Predict the output","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Parameters are the names in the definition: function hikeTime(miles, pace). Arguments are the values you pass in when you call it: hikeTime(6.7, 2.5). Calling a function needs parentheses. Without them, you're just referring to the function, not running it. return sends a value back to wherever the function was called, and ends the function right there. Default parameters fill in missing arguments: function greet(name = \"friend\"). Functions inside objects are called methods, like text.toUpperCase() or console.log()."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Using console.log() where you meant return. The value is printed, but the caller gets undefined. Passing a function call instead of the function: btn.addEventListener(\"click\", go()) runs go right away. Use go with no parentheses. Using a variable declared inside a function from outside it: ReferenceError. Putting code after return and expecting it to run."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"Functions: reusable blocks of codeParameters, defaults, anonymous and arrow functions, scope. Build your own functionA step-by-step exercise. Function return valuesHow return works, and why it matters."}]},{"slug":"js-objects-json","num":"6.9","title":"Objects & JSON","desc":"Group values under named keys, read nested data, and spot invalid JSON.","props":["{ key: value }","obj.key","JSON.parse()"],"intro":"An object groups related values under named keys, such as a trail's name, length and location. JSON is the same idea written as text, and it's how most data travels between websites and servers.","more":"Click a key to read it Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-dot-or-brackets","title":"Dot or brackets?","text":"When the key is stored in a variable (for example, chosen from a dropdown), you have to use square brackets. A dot always looks for a key with that exact name. const key = \"name\" \"miles\" \"open\" \"rating\" Part 3"},{"id":"sec-json-checker","title":"JSON checker","text":"JSON looks like a JavaScript object but has stricter rules. Edit the text, or click a button to make a common mistake. The status uses the browser's own JSON.parse(). Break it Reset to valid JSON Keys and strings in \"double quotes\" No trailing commas No comments No functions or undefined Allowed: strings, numbers, true, false, null, arrays, objects Try it"},{"id":"sec-parse-and-stringify","title":"parse and stringify","text":"This is MDN's superheroes example. JSON.parse() turns JSON text into an object you can use. JSON.stringify() turns an object into text, for saving or sending. Practice"},{"id":"sec-check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"An object is a set of key: value pairs in curly braces. Values can be anything, including arrays, other objects and functions (methods). Read values with a dot (hike.miles) or brackets (hike[\"miles\"]). Use brackets when the key is in a variable. Chain through nested data one step at a time: squad.members[1].powers[2]. Add or change a value just by assigning it: hike.rating = 5; JSON is text. JSON.parse(text) makes an object from it; JSON.stringify(obj) makes text from an object. fetch() responses have a .json() method that parses for you."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"hike.key when key is a variable. It looks for a key literally named \"key\". Use hike[key]. Single quotes, trailing commas or comments in a .json file. Any one of them makes the whole file fail to parse. Using JSON text as if it were an object: text.members is undefined until you parse it. Forgetting the commas between properties in an object literal."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"Object basicsDot and bracket notation, methods, and this. Working with JSONThe JSON rules, the superheroes example, parse and stringify."}]},{"slug":"js-dom","num":"7.1","title":"Selecting & Changing Elements","desc":"Find elements with CSS selectors, explore the DOM tree, and change text, classes and styles.","props":["querySelector()","classList","textContent"],"intro":"The browser turns your HTML into a tree of objects called the DOM (Document Object Model). JavaScript finds elements in that tree with the same selectors you use in CSS, then changes them.","more":"Method querySelector() querySelectorAll() getElementById() Selector Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-the-dom-tree","title":"The DOM tree","text":"This is the mini page above as the browser sees it. Matches from your selector are highlighted. Click any node to see how it relates to the others. Part 3"},{"id":"sec-change-what-you-selected","title":"Change what you selected","text":"These act on whatever your selector matched in Part 1. Try them on .featured, then on h2 with querySelectorAll. Reset the mini page Pick a change. Practice"},{"id":"sec-check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"document.querySelector(\".card\") returns the first match, or null if nothing matches. document.querySelectorAll(\".card\") returns every match as a NodeList. Loop over it with for...of. Change text with el.textContent = \"…\". Prefer el.classList.add(), .remove() and .toggle() with styles in your CSS file over setting el.style directly. It keeps CSS and JS separate. In el.style, CSS property names become camelCase: background-color → backgroundColor. Family words: a parent directly contains its children; siblings share a parent; descendants are anything inside, at any depth."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Forgetting the . or #: querySelector(\"card\") looks for a <card> element and returns null. Adding a # with getElementById: getElementById(\"#title\") returns null. It takes the plain id. Treating a NodeList like one element: cards.classList.add() fails. Loop over the list. Selecting elements before they exist, because the script isn't deferred."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"DOM scripting introductionThe DOM tree, selecting, changing and creating elements. Document.querySelector() and Element.classListFull reference."}]},{"slug":"js-events","num":"7.2","title":"Events","desc":"React to clicks, typing and form submits, and see how events bubble up to parents.","props":["addEventListener","preventDefault()","event.target"],"intro":"Events are signals the browser sends when something happens: a click, a key press, a form being submitted. You listen for them with addEventListener and run a function in response.","more":"Listen for e.preventDefault() on submit Clear log Click or double-click me Move the pointer in and out of this box Trail name (type, tab in and out, press Enter) Submit Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-bubbling","title":"Bubbling","text":"When you click the button, the click doesn't stop there. It bubbles up through every ancestor, and each one's click listener runs in turn. This is the setup from MDN's bubbling example. e.stopPropagation() in the button's listener { capture: true } on body and div body div#container Click me Click the button, then click in the div outside the button. Part 3"},{"id":"sec-event-delegation","title":"Event delegation","text":"Instead of adding a listener to each of the 16 tiles, add one listener to their container. Clicks on any tile bubble up to it, and event.target tells you which tile was clicked. Ignore clicks that aren't on a tile Click some tiles, then click in a gap between them. Reset tiles Practice"},{"id":"sec-check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"el.addEventListener(\"click\", handler): the event type as a string, then the function to run. Pass the function itself, without (). The handler receives an event object, usually named e or event. e.target is the element the event happened on. e.preventDefault() stops the browser's default action: a form reloading the page, or a link navigating. Useful types: click, input (every change to a field), change, submit, keydown, focus, blur, mouseover, mouseout. Events bubble from the target up through its ancestors. Delegation uses this: one listener on a parent handles all its children, even ones added later. Remove a listener with removeEventListener, passing the same named function."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"addEventListener(\"click\", go()) runs go immediately. Pass go. \"onclick\" as the event name. The type is just \"click\". Inline HTML handlers like onclick=\"…\". MDN says never to use them: they mix languages and are often blocked by security settings. Listening for click on a form's submit button instead of submit on the form, which misses submissions made by pressing Enter. Forgetting preventDefault(), so the page reloads and your changes vanish."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"Introduction to eventsaddEventListener, the event object and preventDefault. Event bubblingBubbling, capture, target vs currentTarget, and delegation. Element eventsA list of event types."}]},{"slug":"js-create-elements","num":"7.3","title":"Creating & Removing Elements","desc":"Build MDN's shopping list: create elements, add them to the page, and remove them.","props":["createElement()","append()","remove()"],"intro":"JavaScript can build new elements and add them to the page, then take them away again. This shopping list is MDN's DOM scripting exercise. Add and delete items and watch which lines of code run.","more":"Try a mistake Leave out event.preventDefault() Forget list.appendChild(listItem) Use innerHTML instead of textContent Add a few items, then turn on a mistake and add another. For the innerHTML one, try typing <b>Milk</b>. My shopping list Enter a new item Add item Add an item. Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-created-isn-t-the-same-as-on-the-page","title":"Created isn't the same as on the page","text":"createElement() makes an element in memory. It doesn't show up anywhere until you attach it to something that's already on the page. Next line ▸ Restart In memory (not on the page) On the page Trail mix Water Practice"},{"id":"sec-check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"Make an element: const li = document.createElement(\"li\"); Fill it: li.textContent = \"Milk\";, li.classList.add(\"item\"); Put it on the page: list.appendChild(li), or list.append(li) (which can also take text). prepend() adds at the start. Take it off: li.remove(). (MDN's exercise uses the older list.removeChild(li), which works the same.) Appending an element that is already on the page moves it. Use cloneNode(true) to make a copy. Use textContent for anything a person typed. It shows the text exactly as typed and never runs it as HTML."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Creating an element but never appending it, so nothing appears and there's no error. Using innerHTML with user input. Someone can type HTML that runs code on your page (cross-site scripting). Forgetting preventDefault() on the form, so the page reloads and the list is wiped. Adding the delete listener to the wrong thing, such as the whole list instead of each item's button."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"Active learning: a dynamic shopping listThe original exercise, step by step. Document.createElement() and innerHTML securityWhy textContent is safer."}]},{"slug":"js-fetch","num":"7.4","title":"Fetching Data","desc":"Load JSON from a server with fetch(), handle 404s and failures, and update the page.","props":["fetch()","async / await","response.ok"],"intro":"fetch() asks a server for data, usually JSON, and lets you update part of the page without reloading it. The answer takes time to arrive, so your code has to wait for it and be ready for things to go wrong.","more":"What happens on the server Code style .then() async / await Send request ▸ Boulder trails Press Send request. Network panel Part 2","sections":[{"id":"sec-the-file-being-fetched","title":"The file being fetched","text":"The server sends back plain text in JSON format. response.json() parses it into an array of objects, which the code loops over to build the list. trails.json showTrails() Practice"},{"id":"sec-check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","text":""},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","text":"fetch(url) returns a promise: a placeholder for a value that will arrive later. .then() runs when it does, and .catch() runs if something fails. async/await is another way to write the same thing. await pauses that function until the promise settles, and try/catch handles errors. Code after fetch() keeps running right away. It doesn't wait for the response. A 404 is not an error to fetch. Check response.ok yourself and throw if it's false. response.json() reads the body and parses it as JSON (it also returns a promise). Pages opened as file:// can't fetch. Use a local server, such as VS Code's Live Server, or test on GitHub Pages."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","text":"Using the data on the line after fetch(). It isn't there yet. Use it inside .then() or after await. Forgetting return before response.json() in a .then(), so the next .then() gets undefined. Skipping the response.ok check, then trying to parse a 404 error page as JSON. Using await outside an async function (only allowed at the top level of a module). Opening the HTML file directly and wondering why every request fails with Failed to fetch."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","text":"Making network requests with JavaScriptThe fetch() promise chain, response.ok and catch. Challenge: Building a house data UIFetch JSON, filter it, and render it. How do you set up a local testing server?Needed for fetch and modules."}]}],"terms":[{"term":"!important","id":"term-important","cat":"css","def":"A flag that makes a declaration beat every normal rule, regardless of specificity. Avoid it: it makes styles hard to override later.","ex":"color: red !important;","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Absolute path","id":"term-absolute-path","cat":"html","def":"A full web address including the domain. Use it to link to other websites.","ex":"https://developer.mozilla.org","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"Accessibility (a11y)","id":"term-accessibility-a11y","cat":"workflow","def":"Making sites usable by everyone, including people using screen readers, keyboards, zoom, or captions. \"a11y\" is short for the 11 letters between a and y.","ex":"","page":"accessibility.html"},{"term":"Alt text","id":"term-alt-text","cat":"html","def":"The alt attribute on an image: a text description read by screen readers and shown if the image fails to load. Use alt=\"\" for decorative images.","ex":"<img src=\"arch.jpg\" alt=\"Royal Arch above the trees\">","page":"images.html"},{"term":"Animation","id":"term-animation","cat":"css","def":"Motion defined with @keyframes that runs by itself, as opposed to a transition, which runs when a style changes.","ex":"animation: pulse 1s infinite;","page":"transitions.html"},{"term":"aspect-ratio","id":"term-aspect-ratio","cat":"css","def":"Keeps an element's width-to-height shape as its size changes.","ex":"aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;","page":"sizing.html"},{"term":"Attribute","id":"term-attribute","cat":"html","def":"Extra information in an element's opening tag, written as name=\"value\".","ex":"<a href=\"about.html\">","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Attribute selector","id":"term-attribute-selector","cat":"css","def":"Selects elements by their attributes, using square brackets.","ex":"a[href$=\".pdf\"]","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"BEM","id":"term-bem","cat":"workflow","def":"A class-naming pattern: Block, Element, Modifier. It makes it clear which classes belong together.","ex":".card__title, .btn--large","page":"organizing-css.html"},{"term":"Block element","id":"term-block-element","cat":"css","def":"An element that starts on a new line and fills the available width, like a div, p or h1.","ex":"display: block;","page":"display.html"},{"term":"Border","id":"term-border","cat":"css","def":"The line around an element's padding. Part of the box model, between padding and margin.","ex":"border: 2px solid gray;","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"border-radius","id":"term-border-radius","cat":"css","def":"Rounds an element's corners. 50% on a square makes a circle.","ex":"border-radius: 8px;","page":"backgrounds-borders.html"},{"term":"box-shadow","id":"term-box-shadow","cat":"css","def":"Draws a shadow around an element's box, set by x and y offset, blur, spread and color.","ex":"box-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgb(0 0 0 / 15%);","page":"shadows.html"},{"term":"box-sizing","id":"term-box-sizing","cat":"css","def":"Decides whether width includes padding and border. border-box is easier to work with, and most stylesheets set it on everything.","ex":"* { box-sizing: border-box; }","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Box model","id":"term-box-model","cat":"css","def":"Every element is a rectangle made of four layers: content, padding, border and margin.","ex":"","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Breakpoint","id":"term-breakpoint","cat":"layout","def":"A screen width where a media query changes the layout.","ex":"@media (min-width: 600px)","page":"media-queries.html"},{"term":"Browser cache","id":"term-browser-cache","cat":"workflow","def":"Saved copies of files your browser keeps to load pages faster. A hard refresh (Ctrl+Shift+R, or Cmd+Shift+R on a Mac) skips it to show your latest changes.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Cascade","id":"term-cascade","cat":"css","def":"The rules the browser uses to choose between conflicting declarations: importance, then specificity, then source order.","ex":"","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Character entity","id":"term-character-entity","cat":"html","def":"A code for a character that would otherwise be read as HTML, or is hard to type.","ex":"&lt;  &amp;  &copy;","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Child","id":"term-child","cat":"html","def":"An element directly inside another element. The outer one is its parent.","ex":"<ul> <li>child</li> </ul>","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Class","id":"term-class","cat":"css","def":"A reusable label added with the class attribute and selected in CSS with a dot. Many elements can share a class.","ex":".trail-card  →  <div class=\"trail-card\">","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"clear","id":"term-clear","cat":"layout","def":"Makes an element start below any floated elements before it.","ex":"clear: both;","page":"floats.html"},{"term":"Combinator","id":"term-combinator","cat":"css","def":"The part of a selector that describes a relationship: space (descendant), > (child), + (next sibling), ~ (later sibling).","ex":".card > a","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Comment","id":"term-comment","cat":"html","def":"Notes in your code that the browser ignores. HTML and CSS use different syntax.","ex":"<!-- HTML -->   /* CSS */","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Contrast ratio","id":"term-contrast-ratio","cat":"workflow","def":"How different text and background colors are in brightness. Body text needs at least 4.5 : 1.","ex":"","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"Cross axis","id":"term-cross-axis","cat":"layout","def":"In Flexbox, the direction perpendicular to the main axis. align-items works along it.","ex":"","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"CSS","id":"term-css","cat":"css","def":"Cascading Style Sheets: the language that controls how HTML looks, from colors and fonts to layout.","ex":"","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"CSS variable","id":"term-css-variable","cat":"css","def":"A reusable value with a name starting with two dashes, read with var(). Also called a custom property.","ex":"--brand: #2340d9;  color: var(--brand);","page":"css-variables.html"},{"term":"Declaration","id":"term-declaration","cat":"css","def":"One property and its value, ending in a semicolon.","ex":"color: navy;","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Descendant","id":"term-descendant","cat":"html","def":"Any element inside another one, at any depth: children, grandchildren and so on.","ex":".card p","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"DevTools","id":"term-devtools","cat":"workflow","def":"Tools built into every browser for inspecting the HTML and CSS of a page. Open with F12, or Cmd+Option+I on a Mac.","ex":"","page":"devtools.html"},{"term":"display","id":"term-display","cat":"css","def":"The property that sets how an element is laid out: block, inline, inline-block, flex, grid or none.","ex":"display: inline-block;","page":"display.html"},{"term":"DOCTYPE","id":"term-doctype","cat":"html","def":"The first line of every HTML file. It tells the browser to use modern standards mode.","ex":"<!DOCTYPE html>","page":"document-structure.html"},{"term":"DOM","id":"term-dom","cat":"js","def":"Document Object Model: the tree of elements the browser builds from your HTML. JavaScript reads and changes the page through it, and DevTools' Elements panel shows it after the browser has repaired any errors.","ex":"document.querySelector(\"h1\")","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Element","id":"term-element","cat":"html","def":"A piece of a web page: an opening tag, its content and a closing tag.","ex":"<p>Hello</p>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"em (unit)","id":"term-em-unit","cat":"css","def":"A length relative to the current element's font size. Nested em sizes multiply.","ex":"padding: 1em;","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"Empty element","id":"term-empty-element","cat":"html","def":"An element with no content and no closing tag. Also called a void element.","ex":"<img>  <br>  <input>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Fallback font","id":"term-fallback-font","cat":"css","def":"The next font in a font-family list, used when the first isn't available. Always end with a generic family like serif or sans-serif.","ex":"font-family: \"Lora\", Georgia, serif;","page":"web-fonts.html"},{"term":"figure / figcaption","id":"term-figure-figcaption","cat":"html","def":"Wraps an image, chart or code sample with its visible caption.","ex":"<figure><img …><figcaption>…</figcaption></figure>","page":"images.html"},{"term":"filter","id":"term-filter","cat":"css","def":"Applies visual effects like blur, grayscale or drop shadows to an element.","ex":"filter: grayscale(1);","page":"shadows.html"},{"term":"Flexbox","id":"term-flexbox","cat":"layout","def":"A layout method for arranging items in one row or column, with control over spacing and alignment.","ex":"display: flex;","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"Float","id":"term-float","cat":"layout","def":"Moves an element to the left or right so text wraps around it.","ex":"float: left;","page":"floats.html"},{"term":"Focus","id":"term-focus","cat":"workflow","def":"The element that keyboard input goes to. Tab moves focus; :focus-visible styles show where it is.","ex":"","page":"pseudo-classes.html"},{"term":"Font stack","id":"term-font-stack","cat":"css","def":"A list of fonts in font-family, tried in order.","ex":"font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;","page":"typography.html"},{"term":"fr (unit)","id":"term-fr-unit","cat":"layout","def":"A fraction of the free space in a grid container.","ex":"grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;","page":"grid.html"},{"term":"Gap","id":"term-gap","cat":"layout","def":"Space between items in a flex or grid container, without margins on each item.","ex":"gap: 16px;","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"GitHub Pages","id":"term-github-pages","cat":"workflow","def":"A free service that publishes the HTML, CSS and JavaScript files in a GitHub repository as a website.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Gradient","id":"term-gradient","cat":"css","def":"A smooth blend between colors, used as a background image.","ex":"linear-gradient(135deg, teal, navy)","page":"backgrounds-borders.html"},{"term":"Grid","id":"term-grid","cat":"layout","def":"A layout method for rows and columns at the same time.","ex":"display: grid;","page":"grid.html"},{"term":"Head","id":"term-head","cat":"html","def":"The part of an HTML document with information about the page, like its title and stylesheet links. Not shown on the page.","ex":"<head> … </head>","page":"document-structure.html"},{"term":"Heading","id":"term-heading","cat":"html","def":"h1 to h6 elements that give a page its outline. Use one h1 and don't skip levels.","ex":"<h2>Conditions</h2>","page":"text-semantics.html"},{"term":"Hex color","id":"term-hex-color","cat":"css","def":"A color written as # followed by pairs of red, green and blue values from 00 to ff.","ex":"#2340d9","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"HSL","id":"term-hsl","cat":"css","def":"A color written as hue, saturation and lightness. Easy to adjust by hand.","ex":"hsl(225 72% 50%)","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"HTML","id":"term-html","cat":"html","def":"HyperText Markup Language: the language that describes the structure and meaning of web page content.","ex":"","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"ID","id":"term-id","cat":"css","def":"A unique name for one element, selected with #. IDs have very high specificity, so prefer classes for styling.","ex":"#featured  →  <div id=\"featured\">","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"index.html","id":"term-index-html","cat":"workflow","def":"The name for a site's home page, or the main page in any folder. Servers open it automatically.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Inheritance","id":"term-inheritance","cat":"css","def":"Some properties, mostly text-related ones like color and font, pass from a parent to its children automatically.","ex":"","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Inline element","id":"term-inline-element","cat":"css","def":"An element that flows within a line of text, like span, a or strong. Width and height don't apply.","ex":"display: inline;","page":"display.html"},{"term":"Inline style","id":"term-inline-style","cat":"css","def":"CSS written in an element's style attribute. It beats stylesheet rules, so avoid it.","ex":"<p style=\"color: red\">","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Label","id":"term-label","cat":"html","def":"Text connected to a form control. Its for attribute matches the input's id.","ex":"<label for=\"email\">Email</label>","page":"forms.html"},{"term":"Landmark","id":"term-landmark","cat":"html","def":"A major page region, like header, nav, main or footer, that screen reader users can jump to.","ex":"","page":"semantic-elements.html"},{"term":"Main axis","id":"term-main-axis","cat":"layout","def":"In Flexbox, the direction items are laid out in, set by flex-direction. justify-content works along it.","ex":"","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"Margin","id":"term-margin","cat":"css","def":"Transparent space outside an element's border that pushes other elements away.","ex":"margin: 0 auto;","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Media query","id":"term-media-query","cat":"layout","def":"A condition that applies CSS only when it's true, usually based on screen width.","ex":"@media (min-width: 900px) { … }","page":"media-queries.html"},{"term":"Mobile first","id":"term-mobile-first","cat":"layout","def":"Writing the phone layout first, then adding min-width media queries for larger screens.","ex":"","page":"mobile-first.html"},{"term":"Nesting","id":"term-nesting","cat":"html","def":"Putting elements inside other elements. Inner ones must close before outer ones.","ex":"<p><strong>…</strong></p>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Normal flow","id":"term-normal-flow","cat":"layout","def":"How elements are laid out with no layout CSS: blocks stack down the page, inline content flows in lines.","ex":"","page":"display.html"},{"term":"object-fit","id":"term-object-fit","cat":"css","def":"Decides how an image fills a box of a different shape: stretch, fit inside, or crop to cover.","ex":"object-fit: cover;","page":"responsive-images.html"},{"term":"Overflow","id":"term-overflow","cat":"css","def":"What happens when content is bigger than its box: visible, hidden, or scrollable.","ex":"overflow: auto;","page":"overflow.html"},{"term":"Padding","id":"term-padding","cat":"css","def":"Space inside an element, between its content and its border.","ex":"padding: 16px;","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Parent","id":"term-parent","cat":"html","def":"The element directly containing another element.","ex":"","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Placeholder","id":"term-placeholder","cat":"html","def":"Hint text shown in an empty input. It disappears when typing starts, so it can't replace a label.","ex":"placeholder=\"you@example.com\"","page":"forms.html"},{"term":"position","id":"term-position","cat":"layout","def":"Controls how an element is placed: static, relative, absolute, fixed or sticky.","ex":"position: absolute;","page":"positioning.html"},{"term":"Property","id":"term-property","cat":"css","def":"What a declaration changes, like color, margin or font-size.","ex":"font-size","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Pseudo-class","id":"term-pseudo-class","cat":"css","def":"A keyword with one colon that selects elements in a state or position.","ex":":hover  :focus-visible  :nth-child(2)","page":"pseudo-classes.html"},{"term":"Pseudo-element","id":"term-pseudo-element","cat":"css","def":"A keyword with two colons that styles part of an element or adds decorative content.","ex":"::before  ::first-letter","page":"pseudo-elements.html"},{"term":"Relative path","id":"term-relative-path","cat":"html","def":"Directions to a file starting from the current file's folder. ../ goes up one folder.","ex":"../images/logo.png","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"rem (unit)","id":"term-rem-unit","cat":"css","def":"A length relative to the root (html) font size, usually 16px. Good for font sizes.","ex":"font-size: 1.25rem;","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"Repository","id":"term-repository","cat":"workflow","def":"A project folder tracked by Git, with its full history of changes. On GitHub, it's often called a repo.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Responsive design","id":"term-responsive-design","cat":"layout","def":"Building pages that adapt to any screen size, using flexible layouts, images and media queries.","ex":"","page":"media-queries.html"},{"term":"Root-relative path","id":"term-root-relative-path","cat":"html","def":"A path starting with /, measured from the top of the domain. Breaks on GitHub Pages project sites.","ex":"/images/logo.png","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"Rule","id":"term-rule","cat":"css","def":"A selector plus its block of declarations. Also called a ruleset.","ex":"h1 { color: navy; }","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Screen reader","id":"term-screen-reader","cat":"workflow","def":"Software that reads a page aloud for people who are blind or have low vision. It relies on semantic HTML and alt text.","ex":"","page":"accessibility.html"},{"term":"Selector","id":"term-selector","cat":"css","def":"The part of a rule that picks which elements to style.","ex":".card h2","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Semantic HTML","id":"term-semantic-html","cat":"html","def":"Choosing elements for what content means, like nav, article or button, rather than how it looks.","ex":"","page":"semantic-elements.html"},{"term":"Sibling","id":"term-sibling","cat":"html","def":"Elements that share the same parent.","ex":"h2 + p","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Source order","id":"term-source-order","cat":"css","def":"Where a rule appears in the CSS. When everything else ties, the later rule wins.","ex":"","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Specificity","id":"term-specificity","cat":"css","def":"A score for how targeted a selector is: IDs beat classes, classes beat elements. Higher specificity wins a conflict.","ex":"#nav a  beats  .link","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"srcset","id":"term-srcset","cat":"html","def":"A list of image files at different widths. The browser downloads the smallest one that will still look sharp.","ex":"srcset=\"trail-400.jpg 400w, trail-800.jpg 800w\"","page":"responsive-images.html"},{"term":"Stylesheet","id":"term-stylesheet","cat":"css","def":"A .css file of rules, linked from the head of each HTML page.","ex":"<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Tag","id":"term-tag","cat":"html","def":"The text in angle brackets that marks where an element starts or ends.","ex":"<p>  </p>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Transition","id":"term-transition","cat":"css","def":"Smoothly animates a change from one style to another, such as on hover.","ex":"transition: background 0.2s ease;","page":"transitions.html"},{"term":"Type selector","id":"term-type-selector","cat":"css","def":"A selector that matches every element of one kind by its tag name.","ex":"p  h2  button","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Unit","id":"term-unit","cat":"css","def":"What a number measures in, like px, rem, %, vw or s. Most values except 0 need one.","ex":"16px  1.5rem  50%","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"URL","id":"term-url","cat":"html","def":"A web address, like https://example.com/trails/index.html.","ex":"","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"User agent stylesheet","id":"term-user-agent-stylesheet","cat":"css","def":"The browser's built-in default styles, such as margins on headings and blue underlined links.","ex":"","page":"devtools.html"},{"term":"Validator","id":"term-validator","cat":"workflow","def":"A tool that checks HTML or CSS against the official rules and reports errors with line numbers.","ex":"","page":"validation.html"},{"term":"Value","id":"term-value","cat":"css","def":"The setting given to a property in a declaration.","ex":"navy, 2rem, auto","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Viewport","id":"term-viewport","cat":"layout","def":"The visible area of the browser window. vw and vh are percentages of it.","ex":"","page":"sizing.html"},{"term":"Viewport meta tag","id":"term-viewport-meta-tag","cat":"html","def":"Tells phones to use the real screen width instead of shrinking a desktop-sized page.","ex":"<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">","page":"document-structure.html"},{"term":"Web font","id":"term-web-font","cat":"css","def":"A font downloaded with the page, for example from Google Fonts, so everyone sees the same typeface.","ex":"","page":"web-fonts.html"},{"term":"Whitespace","id":"term-whitespace","cat":"html","def":"Spaces, tabs and line breaks. In HTML, any run of whitespace shows as a single space.","ex":"","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"z-index","id":"term-z-index","cat":"layout","def":"Controls which positioned elements appear on top when they overlap. Higher numbers come forward.","ex":"z-index: 10;","page":"positioning.html"},{"term":"Client and server","id":"term-client-and-server","cat":"workflow","def":"The browser is the client: it asks for files. The server is the computer that stores the site and sends them back.","ex":"","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"Domain name","id":"term-domain-name","cat":"workflow","def":"The human-friendly name of a server, like github.io. DNS looks up the numeric address behind it.","ex":"trailclub.github.io","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"HTTP status code","id":"term-http-status-code","cat":"workflow","def":"A number the server sends back with every response. 200 means OK, 404 means not found.","ex":"404 Not Found","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"Local server","id":"term-local-server","cat":"workflow","def":"A small web server running on your own computer, like VS Code's Live Server, so you can test pages the way the real web serves them.","ex":"http://127.0.0.1:5500","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"Favicon","id":"term-favicon","cat":"html","def":"The small icon in the browser tab and bookmarks, linked from the head.","ex":"<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.svg\">","page":"head-metadata.html"},{"term":"Meta description","id":"term-meta-description","cat":"html","def":"A one-sentence summary of a page in the head. Search engines may show it under your title.","ex":"<meta name=\"description\" content=\"…\">","page":"head-metadata.html"},{"term":"Open Graph","id":"term-open-graph","cat":"html","def":"og: meta tags that control the preview card when a link is shared in chats and social posts.","ex":"<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://…\">","page":"head-metadata.html"},{"term":"Lossy / lossless","id":"term-lossy-lossless","cat":"workflow","def":"Lossy formats like JPG throw away some detail to make files smaller. Lossless formats like PNG keep every pixel.","ex":"","page":"image-formats.html"},{"term":"SVG","id":"term-svg","cat":"html","def":"Scalable Vector Graphics: an image made of shapes described in code, so it stays sharp at any size. Best for logos and icons.","ex":"<img src=\"logo.svg\" alt=\"Trail Club\">","page":"image-formats.html"},{"term":"WebP","id":"term-webp","cat":"html","def":"A modern image format that's usually smaller than JPG or PNG at the same quality, and supports transparency.","ex":"photo.webp","page":"image-formats.html"},{"term":"iframe","id":"term-iframe","cat":"html","def":"An element that shows another web page inside yours. Used for YouTube videos, maps and other embeds.","ex":"<iframe src=\"…\" title=\"Trail map\"></iframe>","page":"embeds.html"},{"term":"Embed","id":"term-embed","cat":"html","def":"Content from another site shown on your page, usually with an iframe the other site gives you.","ex":"","page":"embeds.html"},{"term":"sizes","id":"term-sizes","cat":"html","def":"Tells the browser how wide an image will display at different window widths, so it can pick the right file from srcset.","ex":"sizes=\"(min-width: 700px) 50vw, 100vw\"","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"picture","id":"term-picture","cat":"html","def":"Wraps several source elements and an img, so the browser can choose a different crop or format.","ex":"<picture><source …><img …></picture>","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"Art direction","id":"term-art-direction","cat":"html","def":"Showing a different crop of an image at different screen sizes, not just a smaller copy.","ex":"","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"Lazy loading","id":"term-lazy-loading","cat":"html","def":"Waiting to download an image or iframe until the visitor scrolls near it.","ex":"loading=\"lazy\"","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"clamp()","id":"term-clamp","cat":"css","def":"A value that grows with the screen but stays between a minimum and a maximum: clamp(min, preferred, max).","ex":"font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);","page":"fluid-sizing.html"},{"term":"min() / max()","id":"term-min-max","cat":"css","def":"min() uses the smallest of its values, max() the largest. min() works like a max-width.","ex":"width: min(640px, 100% - 2rem);","page":"fluid-sizing.html"},{"term":"calc()","id":"term-calc","cat":"css","def":"Does math with mixed units. The + and - need spaces around them.","ex":"width: calc(100% - 2rem);","page":"fluid-sizing.html"},{"term":"Transform","id":"term-transform","cat":"css","def":"Moves, rotates, scales or skews an element after layout, without moving anything around it.","ex":"transform: rotate(10deg) scale(1.1);","page":"transforms.html"},{"term":"transform-origin","id":"term-transform-origin","cat":"css","def":"The point an element rotates and scales around. The default is its center.","ex":"transform-origin: top left;","page":"transforms.html"},{"term":"addEventListener()","id":"term-addeventlistener","cat":"js","def":"Runs a function whenever a certain event happens on an element. Pass the function itself, without parentheses.","ex":"btn.addEventListener(\"click\", openMenu);","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Argument","id":"term-argument","cat":"js","def":"A value you pass into a function when you call it. It fills in one of the function's parameters.","ex":"hikeTime(6.7, 2.5)","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"Array","id":"term-array","cat":"js","def":"An ordered list of values stored under one name. Items are numbered from 0.","ex":"const trails = [\"Mesa\", \"Royal Arch\"];","page":"js-arrays.html"},{"term":"Arrow function","id":"term-arrow-function","cat":"js","def":"A short way to write a function. With one expression and no braces, it returns that value automatically.","ex":"const double = (n) => n * 2;","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"async / await","id":"term-async-await","cat":"js","def":"A way to write code that waits for a promise, such as fetch(), as if it ran top to bottom. await only works inside an async function (or a module).","ex":"const res = await fetch(url);","page":"js-fetch.html"},{"term":"Boolean","id":"term-boolean","cat":"js","def":"A value that is either true or false. Comparisons produce booleans.","ex":"const isOpen = miles < 5;","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"classList","id":"term-classlist","cat":"js","def":"An element's list of classes, with add(), remove() and toggle() methods. The usual way to change how an element looks from JavaScript.","ex":"menu.classList.toggle(\"open\");","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Concatenation","id":"term-concatenation","cat":"js","def":"Joining strings together with +. If either side is a string, + joins instead of adding.","ex":"\"5\" + 5 // \"55\"","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"Conditional","id":"term-conditional","cat":"js","def":"Code that only runs if a condition is true: if, else if and else.","ex":"if (temp < 32) { … } else { … }","page":"js-conditionals.html"},{"term":"console.log()","id":"term-console-log","cat":"js","def":"Prints a value to the browser's console. The quickest way to check what your code is doing.","ex":"console.log(\"score is\", score);","page":"js-errors.html"},{"term":"const","id":"term-const","cat":"js","def":"Declares a variable that can't be reassigned. Use it by default; switch to let only when the value must change.","ex":"const name = \"Ava\";","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"createElement()","id":"term-createelement","cat":"js","def":"Makes a new element in memory. It appears on the page only after you append it somewhere.","ex":"const li = document.createElement(\"li\");","page":"js-create-elements.html"},{"term":"defer","id":"term-defer","cat":"js","def":"A script attribute that downloads the file while the page loads and runs it after the HTML is read, so your code can find every element.","ex":"<script defer src=\"script.js\"></script>","page":"js-intro.html"},{"term":"Event","id":"term-event","cat":"js","def":"A signal that something happened, such as a click, a key press or a form being submitted.","ex":"click, input, submit, keydown","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Event bubbling","id":"term-event-bubbling","cat":"js","def":"After an event fires on an element, it travels up through each ancestor, running their listeners too.","ex":"","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Event delegation","id":"term-event-delegation","cat":"js","def":"Putting one listener on a parent to handle events from all its children, using event.target to tell them apart.","ex":"list.addEventListener(\"click\", (e) => { … });","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"event.target","id":"term-event-target","cat":"js","def":"The element an event actually happened on. Compare event.currentTarget, the element whose listener is running.","ex":"e.target.textContent","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"fetch()","id":"term-fetch","cat":"js","def":"Requests a file or data from a server and returns a promise for the response. Doesn't work on pages opened from file://.","ex":"fetch(\"trails.json\")","page":"js-fetch.html"},{"term":"Function","id":"term-function","cat":"js","def":"A named, reusable block of code. It can take parameters and return a value.","ex":"function greet(name) { return `Hi ${name}`; }","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"Index","id":"term-index","cat":"js","def":"An item's position in an array or a character's position in a string, counting from 0.","ex":"trails[0]","page":"js-arrays.html"},{"term":"JavaScript","id":"term-javascript","cat":"js","def":"The programming language of the web. It adds behavior to pages: responding to clicks, changing content and loading data.","ex":"","page":"js-intro.html"},{"term":"JSON","id":"term-json","cat":"js","def":"JavaScript Object Notation: a text format for data that looks like a JavaScript object, with stricter rules (double quotes, no trailing commas, no comments).","ex":"{ \"name\": \"Mesa\", \"miles\": 6.7 }","page":"js-objects-json.html"},{"term":"let","id":"term-let","cat":"js","def":"Declares a variable whose value can be changed later.","ex":"let score = 0; score = 10;","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"Loop","id":"term-loop","cat":"js","def":"Code that repeats, usually once for each item in a list: for...of, for, or while.","ex":"for (const trail of trails) { … }","page":"js-loops.html"},{"term":"Method","id":"term-method","cat":"js","def":"A function that belongs to an object, called with a dot.","ex":"text.toUpperCase()","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"NaN","id":"term-nan","cat":"js","def":"\"Not a Number\": the result of math that failed, usually from trying to convert text that isn't a number.","ex":"Number(\"abc\") // NaN","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"null / undefined","id":"term-null-undefined","cat":"js","def":"Two ways of saying \"no value\". undefined means nothing was ever set; null means it was set to nothing on purpose. querySelector returns null when nothing matches.","ex":"","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"Object","id":"term-object","cat":"js","def":"A group of related values stored under named keys.","ex":"const hike = { name: \"Mesa\", miles: 6.7 };","page":"js-objects-json.html"},{"term":"Parameter","id":"term-parameter","cat":"js","def":"A name in a function's definition that stands for a value passed in when it's called.","ex":"function hikeTime(miles, pace = 2)","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"preventDefault()","id":"term-preventdefault","cat":"js","def":"Stops the browser's default action for an event, like a form reloading the page or a link navigating away.","ex":"e.preventDefault();","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Promise","id":"term-promise","cat":"js","def":"A placeholder for a value that isn't ready yet, such as a server's response. Use .then() and .catch(), or await, to get the result.","ex":"fetch(url).then((res) => res.json())","page":"js-fetch.html"},{"term":"querySelector()","id":"term-queryselector","cat":"js","def":"Finds the first element that matches a CSS selector, or null. querySelectorAll() finds every match.","ex":"document.querySelector(\".card\")","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Return value","id":"term-return-value","cat":"js","def":"The value a function sends back to where it was called, using return. Without return, it's undefined.","ex":"return miles / pace;","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"Scope","id":"term-scope","cat":"js","def":"Where a variable can be used. Variables declared inside a function or block only exist there.","ex":"","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"String","id":"term-string","cat":"js","def":"Text, written in quotes: \"double\", 'single' or `backticks`.","ex":"\"Royal Arch\"","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"Syntax error","id":"term-syntax-error","cat":"js","def":"A typo in the structure of the code, such as a missing bracket or quote. The whole script stops before it runs.","ex":"SyntaxError: missing ) after argument list","page":"js-errors.html"},{"term":"Template literal","id":"term-template-literal","cat":"js","def":"A string in backticks that can include variables with ${ }.","ex":"`${trail} is ${miles} miles`","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"textContent","id":"term-textcontent","cat":"js","def":"The text inside an element. Setting it replaces the contents with plain text, which is safer than innerHTML for anything a user typed.","ex":"heading.textContent = \"Hello\";","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Truthy / falsy","id":"term-truthy-falsy","cat":"js","def":"How a value behaves in an if. false, 0, \"\", null, undefined and NaN are falsy; everything else is truthy.","ex":"if (name) { … }","page":"js-conditionals.html"},{"term":"typeof","id":"term-typeof","cat":"js","def":"Tells you a value's type as a string.","ex":"typeof \"42\" // \"string\"","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"Variable","id":"term-variable","cat":"js","def":"A named container for a value, declared with let or const.","ex":"const city = \"Boulder\";","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"=== (strict equality)","id":"term-strict-equality","cat":"js","def":"Checks whether two values are the same, including their type. Use it instead of ==.","ex":"\"5\" === 5 // false","page":"js-conditionals.html"}]};
